// query.mn — forensic substrate for the live graph (spec 08)
//
// `mentl query` is the read-only projection of the inference result.
// No mutations. No side effects. Every question reads through
// GraphRead + EnvRead; write attempts fail at handler install
// via spec 01's subsumption gate. The forensic substrate is airtight
// by type.
//
// Every LSP method (Arc F.2) is a Question variant over this same
// effect. There is no separate LSP substrate. JSON-RPC is the
// transport; tentacles are already here.
import types

import graph

import effects

import parser

import infer

import imap

import format

// The structural census (Hβ.query.structural-census — PLAN §11 Phase 0.3):
// ONE projection taking a structural shape, answering count + located
// sites over the program weave — the question the medium could not ask of
// its own source ("how many of my functions are anonymous?", "how much of
// this file is ><?") without grep on emitted WAT (three filed mentl-skips
// in one audit). The shapes serve the named consumers: anonymity (raw
// CsAnonymous, plus the tier's two conviction shapes CsEta and
// CsEffectfulLambda — Phase 2.5 ratchets on the convictions), verb-shape
// (CsVerb carries the PipeKind itself, classified at the grammar boundary
// through show_pipe_kind — the one glyph projection, never a second
// table), and iteration-shape (CsIterationCostume — the audit tier's
// detector living HERE, the census its home, the audit a reader).
// The width past which a declared row stops being an annotation and starts
// being an inventory. EIGHT, and the number is a reading rather than a taste:
// A declared-row term that authors a bare positive name — the inventory
// shape's one test, shared by the census and the audit tier.
fn term_is_bare_positive(term) = match term {
  (name, negated, _) => if negated {
    false
  } else {
    match name {
      ENamed(_) => true,
      _ => false,
    }
  },
}

type CensusShape
  = CsAnonymous
  | CsVerb(PipeKind)
  | CsEta
  | CsEffectfulLambda
  | CsIterationCostume
  | CsWildcardZero
  | CsFailureMask
  | CsPrintInReport
  | CsWildcardFabricates
  | CsUnderscoreRetain
  | CsFlagAsInt
  | CsParallelArrays
  | CsVtableRecord
  | CsEnvFrame
  // The first DECLARED-SURFACE shape, distinct in kind from every shape
  // above it: those ask what the source DOES (a drift silhouette, a
  // judged row, a verb glyph), this asks whether a form SYNTAX declares
  // is written here at all. The selector needs it — §11 tripwire 3 says
  // the board is green on what the wheel does and silent on everything
  // else, so every oracle-blind probe opens by asking which declared
  // surfaces the wheel never writes, and until now that question could
  // only be answered by a grep from outside the medium
  // (`Hβ.query.default-param-census`). Labeled arguments, as-patterns,
  // record rest and named row aliases are the siblings this arm's shape
  // fits; each is one more variant reading its own carrier, not a
  // generalized surface parameter, because their carriers differ.
  | CsDefaultParam
  | CsDeclaredRowHof
  // A SIGNATURE THAT IS A MANIFEST — a declared row past the width where a
  // reader can hold it, which on this wheel means past EIGHT names.
  //
  // A4 (2026-09-27): a signature that authors ANY bare positive name. It
  // began as the WIDE row (measured 2026-09-21 over src/: 132 fns at four
  // effects, 155 at five, then a tail at 13, 14, 16, 17, 18 and nineteen),
  // and the argument was never width. A declared positive name is a
  // RE-DERIVATION — SYNTAX says the declared row is "a CONSTRAINT verified
  // against the row inferred from the body", so each name is a hand-copy of
  // a proven fact and widening one leaf edits every signature above it; it
  // is not INTENT — the Intent Boundary Rule reserves annotations for
  // decisions, and `with Memory + Alloc` on a body that obviously allocates
  // is dictation; and it BURIES THE SIGNAL — `!E` is the crown, and the one
  // negation worth reading is a needle in a haystack the author typed. The
  // positive row is inferred and projected at the address surface; what a
  // signature authors is a decision — a negation, an instance pin, Pure —
  // and `mentl tighten` writes that residue for every clause the judgment
  // narrates (T_RowInventory, T_OverDeclared). The board holds the wheel at
  // the measured count, and it only falls.
  | CsAuthoredPositiveRow
  // The second declared-surface shape: every record pattern, each a
  // structural read of its receiver's row, which is what keys the twin a
  // row-polymorphic receiver's call sites specialize. The count belonged
  // to the medium rather than to the grep that asked it first. Its judged
  // half, the patterns over an open receiver, was a bound held at zero
  // while those offsets came from the pattern's own field indices; it
  // retired on 2026-09-28, when the emit began reading them from the
  // receiver's whole field set under each twin
  // (`Hβ.lower.record-pattern-param-receiver`, closed).
  | CsRecordPattern
  // A string literal whose text contains the needle — the `text` facet's
  // shape, the one reach into what the wheel WRITES rather than what it
  // calls: the emit's WAT is string literals, and no identifier edge reaches
  // a `$global` or an instruction inside one. A literal chunk of an
  // interpolating string is its own literal node, so the walk sees it too.
  | CsText(String)
  // A comment whose prose contains the needle — the `text` facet's twin over
  // what the wheel SAYS: a comment is graph content attached to the node it
  // precedes, so the site is the comment's own span, read off the cell the
  // parse attached. Confessed as a missing facet four times in a day — every
  // audit of the prose went to a grep because no verb read comments.
  //
  // THE NEEDLE IS A TOKEN, NOT A SUBSTRING (G2): an end of the needle that is
  // a word byte must meet a non-word byte in the comment, so a needle naming
  // one representation answers at that name and never inside a longer one
  // (the substring read found V1 inside `RV128`).
  | CsProse(String)
  // A comment of one PROSE CLASS — what SYNTAX §Comments says a comment is not
  // (a place, a lifecycle, archaeology, decoration, a silenced gate), read by
  // class rather than by a needle the reader has to know to ask for. The
  // board bounds each class at its measured count, falling as each module
  // carrying one is touched.
  | CsProseClass(ProseClass)

// The prose classes, each the comment-paradigm law it breaks. The needles
// each class reads live in string literals beside its matcher (prose_needles),
// never in this prose, so the definition does not count itself.
type ProseClass
  // a module coordinate — a file name and a line number, where the
  // declaration's NAME was the edge; a line number rots at the first edit
  // above it
  = PcCoordinate
  // a repository path that names no file — a deleted module was cited for
  // weeks after it was gone
  | PcPathNoFile
  // a hex commit token — history lives in LEDGER and git, not on the node
  | PcCommit
  // a numbered citation of the archived spec corpus, out of the read-path
  | PcSpecCitation
  // what was rather than what is — the lifecycle vocabulary SYNTAX forbids
  | PcLifecycle
  // the archaeology vocabulary of the seed era and its design notes
  | PcArchaeology
  // a decoration run — three or more box-drawing glyphs or equals signs in a
  // row; the declaration's name IS the heading
  | PcBanner
  // the drift audit's suppression marker — a comment that silences a gate,
  // which SYNTAX says a comment never does
  | PcSilencer

// Sum type covering every forensic question `mentl query` can pose.
// Each variant corresponds to one `ask(Q)` arm in `query_default`.
type Question
  // "type of NAME"
  = QTypeOf(String)
  // "type at L:C"
  | QTypeAt(Span)
  // "intent of handle N"
  | QIntentOf(Int)
  // every handle that chases to NFree
  | QUnresolved
  // "subst trace for handle N"
  | QSubstChain(Int)
  // "effects of NAME"
  | QEffects(String)
  // "ownership of NAME"
  | QOwnership(String)
  // "why NAME" — walks reason DAG
  | QWhy(String)
  // "refs of NAME" — for rename
  | QRefsOf(String)
  // "writes of FIELD" — every value written into a handler state field of
  // that name, read off the writers the judgment noted on its init
  | QStateWrites(String)
  // "verification" — pending obligations
  | QVerifyDebt
  // "which handler provides OP"
  // QFlowOf — the IFC tentacle (§4⑥). Reads the existing graph — TRefined,
  // TName, and the row's absent set — to classify NAME's FlowLabel without any
  // new graph state. The security classification IS already in the type;
  // this is the projection that names it. Novel: no other language exposes
  // IFC as a read of its existing type system. `mentl query flow key` answers
  // "what does the graph already know about key's sensitivity?"
  | QHandlerProvider(String)
  | QFlowOf(String)
  // "census <shape>" — count + sites of a structural shape in the weave
  | QCensus(CensusShape)
  // "census" — the roster itself: every shape the census knows, by the name
  // a question asks it with, and its count over this program
  | QCensusRoster
  // "decls" — the decls column's projection: every declaration the
  // judgment noted, located
  | QDecls
  // "where NAME" — the derived-badge projection (Hβ.cli.where-verb):
  // repr width, resume cardinality, fanout schedules. Output, never input.
  // The second field is the module the question is about: a local is
  // found where it is read, and the link's other modules read names of
  // their own ("" asks every module).
  | QWhere(String, String)
  // "variants NAME" — the ADT-roster facet (the lowering stamp's
  // confessed missing projection): the type's constructors with their
  // arities, read from the env's ConstructorScheme registry — the same
  // channel synth's enumeration and the eq sum-leaf read.
  | QVariants(String)
  // "modules" — the weave's own module set, read from the NModule cells
  // the driver mints per range. The DAG is proved on every invocation and
  // was projectable by nobody: asking which modules an entry pulls meant a
  // shell closure loop over `grep '^import'`, re-deriving by name what the
  // graph holds by edge.
  | QModules
  | QImports
  | QUnreferenced
  // "unreachable" — unreferenced's transitive face: the fn decls the entry
  // cannot reach over the frees DAG
  | QUnreachable
  | QPerforms
  | QOrphanClaims
  // "smt" — every undischarged obligation rendered as an SMT-LIB assertion,
  // the exact text Phase 8.3's `~> verify_smt` handler will hand a solver.
  | QSmt
  // "cost" — what this question cost the medium, in the medium's own
  // facts: modules linked, source lines processed, nodes minted. A wall
  // clock is a HOST fact and varies per run, so it can be reported and
  // never ratcheted; these three are graph reads and are identical every
  // time, so a gate can refuse a landing that makes the medium process
  // more source to answer the same question.
  | QCost
  // "ghosts" — the cells the parser minted that the judgment never bound,
  // located: what the caret reads past, counted so the minting can stop
  | QGhosts
  | QUnknown(String)

type QueryResult
  = QRType(Ty, Reason)
  | QRIntent([(EffName, Bool, EConn)])
  // [(name, ty, free-tvars)]
  | QRUnresolved([(String, Ty, [Ty])])
  // [(handle, GNode)]
  | QRChain([(Int, GNode)])
  | QREffects(EffRow)
  | QROwnership([TParam])
  | QRWhy(Reason)
  // The refs sites as (module, span) PAIRS — the same shape the census and
  // decls facets already answer in. This facet was the last holdout carrying
  // a BARE span, and a bare span is not an address: `at 3090:3` names a line
  // in no file, so reading it costs a grep to find out whose line it is (paid
  // twice in one session). Worse, the dedup compared bare spans, so two refs
  // at one line:col in DIFFERENT modules were one entry by construction.
  // The handle knows its module; the collector was discarding it.
  | QRRefs([(String, Span)])
  | QRVerifyDebt([(Int, Span, Predicate, Reason)])
  // THE SAME OBLIGATIONS, PROJECTED AS SMT-LIB — `mentl query <f> "smt"`.
  // One read (`verify_debt()`), two renders: `QRVerifyDebt` speaks the
  // predicate in the medium's own voice for a person, and this one speaks it
  // in the solver's, which is exactly what Phase 8.3's handler swap will
  // hand to Z3/CVC5. The payload is identical because the FACT is.
  //
  // It exists because the serializer did, unreached: fourteen decls in
  // src/verify.mn rendered a complete SMT-LIB assertion and NOTHING called
  // them — `mentl query src/main.mn unreachable` named the whole family.
  // A serializer with no caller is a claim, not a capability; giving it a
  // projection is what turns it back into one, and it means 8.3 arrives with
  // this half already exercised instead of re-derived.
  | QRSmt([(Int, Span, Predicate, Reason)])
  // every handler declaring an arm for the op, with the arm's address
  | QRHandlerProvider([(String, String)])
  // IFC projection — the FlowLabel the type already proves
  | QRFlowLabel(FlowLabel, Reason)
  // census label + the located sites (count = len)
  // the sites as (module, span) PAIRS: a local span names a line, and the
  // module names whose line it is — the two halves the weave used to fuse
  // into one global coordinate and three readers had to take apart again.
  | QRCensus(String, [(String, Span)])
  // (the name a question asks it with, its label, its count) per shape
  | QRCensusRoster([(String, String, Int)])
  // every name the judged declarations declare (count = len), each beside
  // its declaration's (module, span) site — a roster that answered
  // addresses alone could not say what it listed.
  | QRDecls([(String, (String, Span))])
  // the weave's module paths, in weave order (count = len)
  | QRModules([String])
  // (importing module, imported path, is any name it brings referenced here)
  | QRImports([(String, String, Bool)])
  | QRUnreferenced([(String, String, Int)])
  // (module, fn name, reached-from-entry: 1 or 0)
  | QRUnreachable([(String, String, Int)])
  | QRPerforms([(String, String, Int, Int)])
  | QROrphanClaims([(String, String, Int)])
  // modules linked, source lines processed, nodes minted
  | QRCost(Int, Int, Int)
  // (module, span) of each cell minted and never judged
  | QRGhosts([(String, Span)])
  // the derived badges (plain lines) + the fanout sites
  | QRWhere([String], [WhereSite])
  | QRError(String)

// A fanout site as `where` reports it: how its branches are written and how
// many it spawns, the schedule its own frame installs, the callers whose
// installs demand it threaded (only where its own frame installs none),
// and where it stands.
type WhereSite = WhereSite({schedule: Schedule, shape: SiteShape, span: Span, vias: [String]})

// A site's branches: a `><` or `<|` with the edges its boundary holds, or
// the sequence fanout's one branch per element, counted at run time.
type SiteShape = Branches(FanOwnership, Int) | PerElement

// The single-op effect that routes every forensic question through
// `query_default`. LSP methods (Arc F.2) are `Question` variants
// over this same effect — no separate LSP substrate.
effect Query {
  ask(Question) -> QueryResult
}

// Read-only projection over the live graph and env. No handler-local
// state; all data arrives through `GraphRead + EnvRead`. Pure
// observation — write attempts fail at handler install via the row
// subsumption gate.
handler query_default {
  ask(q) => match q {
    QTypeOf(name) => match env_lookup(name) {
      None => resume(QRError("not found: {name}")),
      Some(entry) => match entry {
        (scheme, reason, _kind) => resume(QRType(scheme |> judgment_ty |> chase_type_deep, reason)),
      },
    },
    QTypeAt(span) => // One live cursor read: tightest enclosing span → handle → the node's
    // bound type, chased deep. No throwaway TName("Unknown") chase — the
    // graph_chase below IS the live read; an unbound node's type IS its own
    // TVar (the chase terminated there), so the projection is total over the
    // NodeKind without a fabricated "Unresolved" name.
    match graph_snapshot() {
      Graph(_, span_index) => match find_tightest(span_index, span, None, 999999999) {
        None => resume(QRError("no enclosing span found in AST index")),
        Some(handle) => match graph_chase(handle) {
          GNode(kind, reason) => match kind {
            NBound(ty) => resume(QRType(chase_type_deep(ty), reason)),
            NFree(_) => resume(QRType(TVar(handle), reason)),
            NRowBound(_) => resume(QRType(TVar(handle), reason)),
            NRecordRowBound(_, _) => resume(QRType(TVar(handle), reason)),
            NRowFree(_) => resume(QRType(TVar(handle), reason)),
            NErrorHole(_) => resume(QRType(TVar(handle), reason)),
          },
        },
      },
    },
    QIntentOf(handle) => // The authored intent row is a field on the FnStmt node the handle
    // names — read it LIVE (graph_node_body), never a parallel
    // intent ledger re-found by scan (Carried-Truth). A non-fn node
    // carries no authored intent.
    match graph_node_body(handle) {
      NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, effs, _)) => resume(QRIntent(effs)),
      _ => // drift-audit: ignore
      resume(QRError("no authored intent at handle {int_to_str(handle)}")),
    },
    QUnresolved => match env_snapshot() {
      Env(entries) => resume(QRUnresolved(find_unresolved(entries))),
    },
    QSubstChain(handle) => resume(QRChain(walk_chain(handle, 0))),
    QEffects(name) => match env_lookup(name) {
      None => resume(QRError("not found: {name}")),
      Some(entry) => match entry {
        (scheme, _, _) => match chase_type_deep(judgment_ty(scheme)) {
          TFun(_, _, row) => resume(QREffects(row)),
          _ => resume(QRError("{name} is not a function")),
        },
      },
    },
    QOwnership(name) => match env_lookup(name) {
      None => resume(QRError("not found: {name}")),
      Some(entry) => match entry {
        (scheme, _, _) => match chase_type_deep(judgment_ty(scheme)) {
          TFun(params, _, _) => resume(QROwnership(params)),
          _ => resume(QRError("{name} is not a function")),
        },
      },
    },
    QWhy(name) => match env_lookup(name) {
      None => resume(QRError("not found: {name}")),
      Some(entry) => match entry {
        (_, reason, _) => // Walk the reason to ROOT — why_expand chases each Fresh(id) through
        // the graph to its resolved reason, so intent is walkable, not a chain
        // ending in dangling unification vars. The engine existed (mentl.mn:437)
        // and only why_from_handle called it; the CLI `why` now performs it too.
        resume(QRWhy(why_expand(reason, 0))),
      },
    },
    QRefsOf(name) => // Every reference to NAME is a VarRef node in the one graph (the parser
    // wrote each use into the program weave). refs-of is the projection of
    // the inbound use-edges: scan the weave for NExpr(VarRef(name)) and
    // collect each node's span — read live, never an "AST index required"
    // surrender. This IS rename: the (module, span) sites are exactly the
    // edit set, and a rename needs the module half — an edit set that names
    // lines without naming files edits nothing.
    resume(QRRefs(refs_of_name(name))),
    QStateWrites(field) => match state_write_lines(field) {
      [] => resume(QRError("no handler declares a state field `{field}`")),
      lines => resume(QRWhere(lines, [])),
    },
    QDecls => resume(QRDecls(flat_map(decl_names_at, decl_handles()))),
    QVerifyDebt => {
      let debt = verify_debt()
      resume(QRVerifyDebt(debt))
    },
    QSmt => // The same bank, handed to the render that speaks solver. Nothing is
    // re-derived here and nothing may be: a second walk that collected the
    // obligations again would be the violation this facet's own subject —
    // the unreached serializer — was a symptom of.
    resume(QRSmt(verify_debt())),
    QHandlerProvider(op_name) => // The handler stack IS graph content (PLAN §2 — "the handler chain IS
    // the capability stack"): each handler decl is a HandlerDeclStmt whose
    // arms name the ops it provides. Which handlers provide OP are the decls
    // with an arm for it, each answered at the arm's own line.
    resume(QRHandlerProvider(handlers_providing_op(op_name))),
    QFlowOf(name) => // The IFC tentacle: the FlowLabel NAME carries, read over the graph
    // with no state of its own (`name_flow_label`).
    match env_lookup(name) {
      None => resume(QRError("not found: {name}")),
      Some(entry) => match entry {
        (scheme, reason, _kind) => resume(QRFlowLabel(name_flow_label(scheme), reason)),
      },
    },
    QCensus(shape) => resume(QRCensus(census_label(shape), census_sites(shape))),
    QCensusRoster => resume(QRCensusRoster(census_roster() |> map(roster_row))),
    QWhere(name, scope) => resume(where_badges(name, scope)),
    QVariants(tname) => resume(variants_of(tname)),
    QModules => // A module SET, rendered in the order of its names: the cells arrive in
    // mint order, which a resident session's re-judged modules leave
    // behind the ones it kept, so the order had been the history of the
    // session rather than a fact about the program.
    resume(QRModules(module_paths() |> sort)),
    QImports => resume(QRImports(import_report(module_cells()))),
    QUnreferenced => resume(QRUnreferenced(unreferenced_report(module_cells()))),
    QUnreachable => resume(QRUnreachable(reach_report(module_cells()))),
    QPerforms => resume(QRPerforms(effect_report(module_cells()))),
    QOrphanClaims => resume(QROrphanClaims(orphan_claim_report(module_cells()))),
    QCost => resume(QRCost(len(module_cells()), weave_line_extent(), graph_next())),
    QGhosts => resume(QRGhosts(ghost_sites())),
    QUnknown(q) => resume(QRError("unknown query: {q}")),
  },
}

// The label a name carries: what its type names — a declaration's
// return, a nominal type's predicate, a row's confinement — joined, for a
// value binding, with its VALUE's label read along the edges the value
// flowed (a parameter's precondition, a let's annotation, a callee's
// declared return). The type alone read every refined value Public once
// the class held a shape (P0).
fn name_flow_label(scheme) = {
  let ty = judgment_ty(scheme)
  let by_type = ty |> chase_type_deep |> query_flow_label
  match ty {
    TVar(b) => flow_label_join(by_type, binder_flow_label(b)),
    _ => by_type,
  }
}

fn chase_type_deep(ref ty) = match ty {
  TVar(h) => match graph_chase(h) {
    GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
      NBound(inner) => chase_type_deep(inner),
      _ => ty,
    },
  },
  TList(elem) => TList(chase_type_deep(elem)),
  TTuple(elems) => TTuple(chase_list_deep(elems)),
  TFun(params, ret, eff) => TFun(chase_params_deep(params), chase_type_deep(ret), eff),
  TName(name, args) => TName(name, chase_list_deep(args)),
  TRecord(fields) => TRecord(chase_fields_deep(fields)),
  TRecordOpen(fields, v) => mk_record_open(chase_fields_deep(fields), v),
  TRefined(base, pred) => TRefined(chase_type_deep(base), pred),
  TCont(input, answer, disc, world) => TCont(chase_type_deep(input), chase_type_deep(answer), disc, world),
  TAlias(name, inner) => TAlias(name, chase_type_deep(inner)),
  _ => ty,
}

// Order-preserving BY the vocabulary form (Hβ.query.param-render-reversed's
// root): the last/drop_last-prepend walks rebuilt every list REVERSED, so
// `type of pair` answered `(beta, alpha)` for a declared (alpha, beta) —
// the voice lying about order at the exact projection that must replace
// reading the source. map preserves; declaration order is the truth. This
// private deep-chase family is one of the nineteen Ty descents the
// traversal-is-a-handler peer consolidates; its one home arrives there.
fn chase_list_deep(ref tys) = map(chase_type_deep, tys)

fn chase_params_deep(ref params) with !Mutate =
  map((p) => param_with_ty(p, chase_type_deep(param_ty(p))), params)

fn chase_fields_deep(ref fields) = map({ (name, ty) => (name, chase_type_deep(ty)) }, fields)

// Collect all env entries whose type still chases to a free `TVar`.
// Returned by `QUnresolved` — the set of names with incomplete types
// after inference, in env order (the reversing walk was the
// param-render-reversed class's fourth face).
fn find_unresolved(ref entries) with !Mutate =
  entries |> filter({ (_, scheme, _, _) => has_free_tvar(judgment_ty(scheme)) }) |> map({ (name, scheme, _, _) => (name, judgment_ty(scheme), []) })

fn has_free_tvar(ref ty) = match ty {
  TVar(h) => match graph_chase(h) {
    GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
      NFree(_) => true,
      NBound(inner) => has_free_tvar(inner),
      _ => false,
    },
  },
  TList(elem) => has_free_tvar(elem),
  TTuple(elems) => has_free_tvar_list(elems),
  TFun(params, ret, _) => has_free_tvar(ret) || has_free_tvar_params(params),
  TName(_, args) => has_free_tvar_list(args),
  TRecord(fields) => has_free_tvar_fields(fields),
  TRecordOpen(fields, _) => has_free_tvar_fields(fields),
  TRefined(base, _) => has_free_tvar(base),
  TCont(input, answer, _, _) => has_free_tvar(input) || has_free_tvar(answer),
  TAlias(_, inner) => has_free_tvar(inner),
  _ => false,
}

fn has_free_tvar_list(ref tys) with !Mutate =
  if len(tys) == 0 { false } else { has_free_tvar(last(tys)) || has_free_tvar_list(drop_last(tys)) }

fn has_free_tvar_params(ref params) = if len(params) == 0 {
  false
} else {
  has_free_tvar(param_ty(last(params))) || has_free_tvar_params(drop_last(params))
}

fn has_free_tvar_fields(ref fields) = if len(fields) == 0 {
  false
} else {
  match last(fields) {
    (_, ty) => has_free_tvar(ty) || has_free_tvar_fields(drop_last(fields)),
  }
}

// Walk every substitution hop from a starting handle to its terminal.
// Returned by `QSubstChain` — useful for tracing cross-module `TVar`
// resolution.
fn walk_chain(handle, depth) = if depth > 50 || handle < 0 {
  []
} else {
  let node = graph_chase(handle)
  match node {
    GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
      NBound(ty) => match ty {
        TVar(next) => [(handle, node)] ++ walk_chain(next, depth + 1),
        _ => [(handle, node)],
      },
      _ => [(handle, node)],
    },
  }
}

// ─── Live use-edge projection — refs-of (rename) ───────────────────
//
// Every judged reference to NAME is noted name → handle in the graph's
// refs column at the one writer (infer_var_ref). The projection reads
// that column — O(refs), never O(graph) — and walks it from the TAIL
// because the column prepends (newest first): oldest-first preserves
// the scan-era answer order and its first-seen span dedup.
fn refs_of_name(name) with !Mutate = map({ (m, sp, _h) => (m, sp) }, ref_sites_of(name))

// The same sites as their HANDLES — what a reader that measures nearness
// between positions needs (the proposer's rank), one per source site.
fn ref_handles_of(name) with !Mutate = map({ (_m, _sp, h) => h }, ref_sites_of(name))

fn ref_sites_of(name) with !Mutate = {
  let hs = graph_refs_at(name)
  collect_ref_sites(hs, len(hs) - 1, make_list(0))
}

fn collect_ref_sites(ref hs, i, acc) with !Mutate = if i < 0 {
  acc
} else {
  // A zero span is a synthetic mint (a pre-registration ghost),
  // not a source site — the located answer skips it. A site already
  // collected is the SAME source site re-minted by a later judgment
  // generation (the converged rounds re-parse; one reference
  // rendered four times before this dedup) — the reference's
  // identity is its source location, so it enters once. The RAW read
  // (the 2.0 walk law at its sibling): a noted ref's span is its OWN
  // parse site, never the union-find root's reason — the chased read
  // greened only because VarRef roots happened to retain Located
  // reasons, an accident-invariant this swap retires.
  //
  // A SOURCE LOCATION IS (MODULE, SPAN), NOT A SPAN. The handle carries
  // its module — graph_module_of is the same read decl_site_of makes for
  // the decls column — and this collector was dropping it, which made the
  // dedup below compare two different files' line 42 as one site. The
  // identity the comment above claims is only an identity once the module
  // is in it.
  let sp = span_of_node_raw(hs[i])
  let m = module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(hs[i]))
  if sp == span_zero() || site_in(acc, m, sp, 0) {
    collect_ref_sites(hs, i - 1, acc)
  } else {
    collect_ref_sites(hs, i - 1, push(acc, (m, sp, hs[i])))
  }
}

// ─── The import edges, read for what they actually carry ────────────
//
// An import is DEAD when nothing the imported module declares is
// referenced from inside the importing one. Every term of that is already
// in the graph: module_cells gives each module its path, its span and its
// decls; an ImportStmt among those decls IS the edge; and graph_refs_at
// answers each declared name's reference sites in O(refs). So the read is
// a fold over edges, never a scan of text — which matters, because the
// text answer cannot tell a used name from one mentioned in a comment,
// and every dead import found on 2026-09-04 hid behind exactly that.
//
// What those three lines cost while nobody could see them: lib/lists,
// lib/strings and lib/threading each imported the compiler's own types
// module and used nothing
// from it, which put the compiler's own ~170 op names into the namespace
// of every program that touches a list. A user's `effect Backtrack {
// abort() }` lost silently to the compiler's Abort. Deleting them took
// that module out of a bare weave and more than halved the prelude floor.
//
// The honest limit, stated because a reader will otherwise trust this
// further than it goes: imports are TRANSITIVE, so an edge carrying no
// referenced name of its own may still be the conduit by which a third
// module arrives. This facet reports what it measured — the direct
// reference — and says nothing about the conduit; `Hβ.driver.link-is-
// reachability` is the judgment that decides those.
// The whole report: every module's every import, paired with whether this
// module references anything that import brings. One pass over the cells,
// one O(1) refs probe per declared name.
// Both stages take their configuration FIRST and the flowing datum last, so
// `import_rows_join(cells)` and `import_verdict(path, extent, cells)` are
// products with their remaining fields as holes — the fold and the map fill
// them. The Stage Law's own payoff: no lambda to write, and none for the
// anonymity tier to convict.
fn import_report(cells) with !Mutate = fold([], import_rows_join(cells), cells)

fn import_rows_join(cells, acc, cell) with !Mutate = acc ++ import_rows_for(cell, cells)

fn import_rows_for(cell, cells) with !Mutate = match cell {
  (_h, path, extent, _) => map(import_verdict(path, extent, cells), import_edges_of(cell)),
}

// The per-edge verdict: the importing module, the path it names, and whether
// anything that path brings is referenced here.
fn import_verdict(path: String, extent, cells, imported) with !Mutate =
  (path, imported, import_is_used(imported, extent, cells, 0))

// Does the named module bring any name this extent references? A module the
// weave does not carry answers False for its edge — never a fabricated True
// that would hide a real dead import.
fn import_is_used(imported: String, extent, cells, i) with !Mutate = if i >= len(cells) {
  false
} else {
  match cells[i] {
    (_h, p, _, _) => if module_tail_eq(p, imported) {
      any_name_referenced_in(module_declared_names(cells[i]), extent, 0)
    } else {
      import_is_used(imported, extent, cells, i + 1)
    },
  }
}

// An import names a PATH (`dsp/clock`); a module cell names its resolved
// file. They agree on the last segment, which is the module's identity.
fn module_tail_eq(a: String, b: String) = last_segment(a) == last_segment(b)

fn last_segment(s: String) = match split("/", s) {
  segs => if len(segs) == 0 { s } else { last(list_to_flat(segs)) },
}

fn import_edges_of(cell) = match cell {
  (_, _, _, decls) => fold([], import_edge_join, decls),
}

fn import_edge_join(acc, d) = match d {
  N(NStmt(ImportStmt(p)), _, _) => push(acc, p),
  _ => acc,
}

// The names a module DECLARES, read from its own decl list — the same
// four claim kinds register_one_op weighs: fns, types with their ctors,
// effects with their ops, handlers.
fn module_declared_names(cell) = match cell {
  (_, _, _, decls) => fold([], decl_names_join, decls),
}

fn decl_names_join(acc, d) = acc ++ decl_names_of(d)

fn decl_names_of(d) = match d {
  N(NStmt(s), _, _) => match s {
    FnStmt(n, _, _, _, _) => [n],
    TypeDefStmt(n, ctors) => [n] ++ map({ (cn, _, _) => cn }, ctors),
    EffectDeclStmt(n, _, ops) => [n] ++ map({ (on, _, _, _) => on }, ops),
    HandlerDeclStmt(n, _, _, _, _, _) => [n],
    _ => [],
  },
  _ => [],
}

// Is any of `names` referenced from within `extent`? The refs column is
// name-keyed, so this is one O(1) probe per name plus its own sites.
fn any_name_referenced_in(names, extent, i) with !Mutate = if i >= len(names) {
  false
} else if refs_inside(graph_refs_at(names[i]), extent, 0) {
  true
} else {
  any_name_referenced_in(names, extent, i + 1)
}

fn refs_inside(hs, extent, i) with !Mutate = if i >= len(hs) {
  false
} else {
  let sp = span_of_node_raw(hs[i])
  if sp != span_zero() && span_overlaps(extent, sp) { true } else { refs_inside(hs, extent, i + 1) }
}

// ─── The declarations, read for what actually reaches them ──────────
//
// A declaration is UNREFERENCED when every reference to its name falls
// inside its own declaration — a fn only its own recursion calls, a
// constructor nothing builds, an effect op no arm performs. This is the
// import read one altitude down and turned around: that one asks whether
// any name an edge BRINGS is referenced INSIDE the importing extent; this
// one asks whether any site referencing a declared name lies OUTSIDE the
// declaring one. Same two columns, same O(1) probe, opposite direction.
//
// Nine dead fns came out of src/ on 2026-09-04, found by grepping each
// name and reading the hits by hand. That method costs one pass per name
// and cannot tell a call from a mention in a comment — the same blindness
// that hid three dead imports for weeks. This is one pass over the decls
// the weave already carries.
//
// The honest limits, stated because a reader will otherwise take this
// further than it goes:
//   · `main` is the entry. Nothing references it; it is not dead.
//   · UNREFERENCED is not unreachable. It measures the direct reference,
//     exactly as the import read does, and says nothing about what the
//     program needs — `Hβ.driver.link-is-reachability` is that judgment.
//   · A name reached through some edge the graph does not draw as a
//     reference reads unreferenced here. That over-report is not noise to
//     tune away; it NAMES the edge the graph is not drawing.
fn unreferenced_report(cells) with !Mutate = fold([], decl_ref_rows_join, cells)

fn decl_ref_rows_join(acc, cell) with !Mutate = acc ++ decl_ref_rows_for(cell)

fn decl_ref_rows_for(cell) with !Mutate = match cell {
  (_h, path, _, decls) => fold([], decl_ref_row_join(path), decls),
}

fn decl_ref_row_join(path: String, acc, d) with !Mutate =
  acc ++ map(decl_ref_verdict(path, decl_extent(d)), decl_names_of(d))

// The per-name verdict: the declaring module, the name, and how many of its
// reference sites lie outside its own declaration. Zero is the finding.
fn decl_ref_verdict(path: String, extent, name: String) with !Mutate =
  (path, name, count(ref_is_outside(extent), graph_refs_at(name)))

fn decl_extent(d) with Pure = match d {
  N(_, sp, _) => sp,
}

fn decl_handle(d) with Pure = match d {
  N(_, _, h) => h,
}

// ─── unreachable — unreferenced's transitive face ───────────────────
//
// UNREFERENCED is one hop: a fn kept alive only by other dead fns reads
// referenced. When the wheel's second judgment pass came out (2026-09-17)
// its 32 dead fns were found by grepping the emitted WAT for what the
// emitter dropped — a projection this verb was one facet from holding,
// and the grep is the confession this facet answers. The edges are the
// frees DAG the judgment already orders by (collect_free_vars, the walk
// scc_groups takes its binding groups from), walked from `main` exactly
// as emit's reachable_from_main walks it; a module without main is the
// library path, where every decl is a root and nothing is unreachable.
// Rows are fn decls only — the unit emit keeps or drops. A type reached
// only through a signature has no frees edge; that is unreferenced's
// read, not this walk's.
fn reach_report(cells) = {
  let decls = list_to_flat(fold([], ur_cell_decls, cells))
  let n = len(decls)
  let idx = ur_index(decls, 0, n, smap_new())
  match smap_get(idx, "main") {
    None => [],
    Some(_) => ur_rows(
      decls,
      ur_walk(decls, idx, ["main"] ++ refine_roots(decls), smap_new(), smap_new()),
      0,
      n,
      []
    ),
  }
}

// A REFINEMENT PREDICATE IS A ROOT. Before it was one, this walk reported
// `span_valid` as dead code.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-21, and the measurement is a warning about this whole
// facet's genre: `mentl query src/main.mn unreachable` listed
// `types -> span_valid`, which is the predicate of
// `type ValidSpan = Span where span_valid(self)` — the Intent Boundary type
// the wheel annotates with everywhere. Deleting it on this facet's word would
// have broken every `ValidSpan` in the tree. A dead-code report is a DELETION
// INSTRUCTION, so under-reporting reachability is the one direction it may
// never be wrong in, and it was.
//
// The root cause is one constructor: `free_vars_stmt` (src/parser.mn) answers
// `RefineStmt(_, _, _) => []`, so a predicate's references reach nothing. That
// is the SAME shape as the `HandlerDeclStmt => []` that took the single-pass
// landing's m4 leg to a trap — one arm over in the same match —  and it is
// still live for the judgment's own callee-first DAG ordering, where a
// refinement calling a later-declared fn has no edge.
// `Hβ.parser.refine-stmt-frees-are-empty` carries that half; this is the
// facet's, fixed here because the facet is what people act on.
//
// SEEDED AS ROOTS rather than as edges from the type's uses, deliberately: a
// `where` clause is discharged wherever its type is annotated, and the walk
// cannot cheaply know where. Over-approximating is the safe direction for a
// report whose output is "delete this".
fn refine_roots(decls) = decls |> map(refine_decl_names) |> flatten

fn refine_decl_names(entry) = match entry {
  (_, d) => match d {
    N(NStmt(RefineStmt(_, _, pred)), _, _) => predicate_names(pred),
    _ => [],
  },
}

// The names a Predicate references, walked through the handles it carries —
// the AST lives in the graph, so the predicate holds edges and this follows
// them rather than re-parsing anything.
fn predicate_names(pred) = match pred {
  PTrue => [],
  PCompare(_, l, r) => handle_var_names(l) ++ handle_var_names(r),
  PAnd(a, b) => predicate_names(a) ++ predicate_names(b),
  POr(a, b) => predicate_names(a) ++ predicate_names(b),
  PNot(p) => predicate_names(p),
  PBoolNode(h) => handle_var_names(h),
  PPatternMatch(_, h) => handle_var_names(h),
  PFlowLe(_, _) => [],
  PWithSelf(_, inner) => predicate_names(inner),
  PTotalDiv(_, a, b) => handle_var_names(a) ++ handle_var_names(b),
  PInBounds(recv, idx) => handle_var_names(recv) ++ handle_var_names(idx),
  PProvides(provided) => match provided {
    ProvidedAt(nodes) => nodes |> flat_map(handle_var_names),
    ProvidedBeyond(nodes) => nodes |> flat_map(handle_var_names),
    ProvidedNone => [],
  },
}

fn handle_var_names(h) = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = match body {
    NExpr(VarRef(n)) => [n],
    _ => [],
  }
  here ++ (body_child_handles(body) |> map(handle_var_names) |> flatten)
}

fn ur_cell_decls(acc, cell) = match cell {
  (_h, path, _, ds) => acc ++ map((d) => (path, d), ds),
}

// name -> decl index, every declared name (a ctor or an op leads to its
// declaring node, which has no frees — a harmless leaf).
fn ur_index(ref decls, i, n, idx) = if i >= n {
  idx
} else {
  match decls[i] {
    (_, d) => ur_index(decls, i + 1, n, fold(idx, (m, name) => smap_add(m, name, i), decl_names_of(d))),
  }
}

// The worklist: pop a name, mark it, push the frees of its declaration —
// except a DISPATCHED handler's arms, which run only when something reached
// performs their operation: each arm's frees park under its op's name and are
// released when that name is reached. Walking every arm of a reached handler
// kept two Format operations nothing performs alive, with the whole-graph
// parent scan one of them carried (measured 2026-10-02). A schedule class's
// arms stay reached with the handler — the lowering performs them on a
// fanout's behalf, and no source name ever does — read off the one roster
// the judgment and the lowering classify an install by.
fn ur_walk(ref decls, idx, work, reached, parked) = match work {
  [] => reached,
  [name, ...more] => match smap_get(reached, name) {
    Some(_) => ur_walk(decls, idx, more, reached, parked),
    None => {
      let now = smap_add(reached, name, 1)
      let released = flatten(smap_get_all(parked, name))
      match smap_get(idx, name) {
        None => ur_walk(decls, idx, more ++ released, now, parked),
        Some(i) => match decls[i] {
          (_, d) => match ur_decl_frees(d, now) {
            (frees, park) => ur_walk(decls, idx, more ++ released ++ frees, now, fold(parked, ur_park, park)),
          },
        },
      }
    },
  },
}

// A declaration's frees to walk now, and the arms it parks as (op, frees).
fn ur_decl_frees(d, reached) = match d {
  N(NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(_, _, config, state, arms, _)), _, _) => match projection_of_effects(handler_effect_names(arms)) {
    PDispatch => {
      let cfg = free_vars_param_names(config)
      let in_arms = cfg ++ map({ st => st.name }, state)
      fold(
        (flatten(map({ st => collect_free_vars(st.init, cfg) }, state)), []),
        ur_arm(reached, in_arms, ??, ??),
        arms
      )
    },
    _ => (collect_free_vars(d, []), []),
  },
  _ => (collect_free_vars(d, []), []),
}

fn ur_arm(reached, in_arms, acc, arm) = match acc {
  (now, park) => {
    let frees = collect_free_vars(arm.body, in_arms ++ arm.args)
    match smap_get(reached, handler_arm_op_name(arm)) {
      Some(_) => (now ++ frees, park),
      None => (now, push(park, (handler_arm_op_name(arm), frees))),
    }
  },
}

fn ur_park(parked, entry) = match entry {
  (op, frees) => smap_add(parked, op, frees),
}

fn ur_rows(ref decls, reached, i, n, acc) = if i >= n {
  acc
} else {
  match decls[i] {
    (path, d) => match d {
      N(NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)), _, _) => {
        let hit = match smap_get(reached, name) {
          Some(_) => 1,
          None => 0,
        }
        ur_rows(decls, reached, i + 1, n, push(acc, (path, name, hit)))
      },
      _ => ur_rows(decls, reached, i + 1, n, acc),
    },
  }
}

// ─── The claims prose makes that the refs column refutes ─────────────
//
// A declaration nothing references, carrying a paragraph that explains how
// it is used, is the strongest dead-code signal in the wheel — stronger
// than the bare zero, because someone WROTE DOWN a belief the graph
// disagrees with. Three finds in one session were exactly this shape and
// each cost a hand read:
//
//   BodyContext   "read by LEvPerform's offset arithmetic" — zero readers,
//                 installed in every lowering route.
//   Delta         "the Cursor handler's CursorView.teach field reads
//                 through delta_pick" — no such field, 407 lines deleted.
//   the Synth effect's unperformed candidate gate  its sibling's comment
//                 said the fan "mirrors the sequential arm exactly"; it
//                 did not, and the unreached copy was the drifted one
//                 (deleted with D4, the op and its arm).
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated because the honest limit is the whole
// design: it does not read English. Deciding that "read by LEvPerform" is
// a CLAIM would need to parse prose, and a checker that guesses at meaning
// would be a second, worse comment-ref tier. It reports the structural
// conjunction — zero outside references AND attached prose past a length
// no incidental note reaches — and leaves the reading to a person. The
// comment-ref ratchet already covers the other half at the name layer:
// a BACKTICKED name that resolves nowhere. All three finds above named
// their phantom readers WITHOUT backticks, which is exactly why that
// ratchet stayed at zero through every one of them.
fn orphan_claim_report(cells) with !Mutate = fold([], orphan_rows_join, cells)

// An AUTHORED hole: a `??` the parser minted with a fresh nonzero id and its
// token's span (a syntax-error recovery hole is one too — an absence to fix
// either way). The weave's absent-default NHole(0) and the driver's span-zero
// scaffolding are not positions: there is nothing at 0:0 to render or patch.
fn authored_hole(handle) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(handle) {
  NHole(id) => id != 0 && !span_eq(parse_span_of(handle), span_zero()),
  _ => false,
}

// A cell the parser minted and the judgment never bound: its own reason is
// still the placeholder it was born with once the whole program is judged.
// An authored hole is free on purpose and is not one; a module node is the
// file, not a cell inside it.
fn node_is_ghost(h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NModule(_) => false,
  _ => match graph_reason_at(h) {
    Placeholder(_) => !authored_hole(h),
    _ => false,
  },
}

// The ghosts facet: every current cell nothing judged, located. The caret
// reads past them (`address_bound`); this is the instrument that finds the
// minting that makes each one.
fn ghost_sites() with !Mutate = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(_, span_index) => span_index |> filter(ghost_entry) |> map(ghost_site),
}

fn ghost_entry(entry) with !Mutate = match entry {
  (_, h) => graph_node_current(h) && node_is_ghost(h),
}

fn ghost_site(entry) with !Mutate = match entry {
  (s, h) => (module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(h)), s),
}

fn orphan_rows_join(acc, cell) with !Mutate = acc ++ orphan_rows_for(cell)

fn orphan_rows_for(cell) with !Mutate = match cell {
  (_h, path, _, decls) => fold([], orphan_row_join(path), decls),
}

fn orphan_row_join(path: String, acc, d) with !Mutate = {
  let prose = graph_comment_at(decl_handle(d))
  if byte_len(prose) < orphan_prose_floor() {
    acc
  } else {
    // Parenthesised deliberately: `++` binds tighter than `|>` (§Precedence),
    // so `acc ++ filter(…) |> map(…)` maps over the ACCUMULATOR. Written as
    // a pipe first and caught by the medium in one check.
    acc ++ map(
      orphan_verdict(path, byte_len(prose)),
      filter(name_is_orphaned(decl_extent(d)), decl_names_of(d))
    )
  }
}

fn name_is_orphaned(extent, name: String) with !Mutate =
  count(ref_is_outside(extent), graph_refs_at(name)) == 0

fn orphan_verdict(path: String, prose_bytes, name: String) = (path, name, prose_bytes)

// The floor is a length, not a judgment: below it a comment is a label
// ("// the fd", "// see above") and its absence of readers says nothing.
// 240 bytes is roughly three full lines of prose — the point at which
// someone was explaining rather than labelling. Every one of the three
// finds carried far more; the number is a threshold to tune against
// measurements, never a claim about what a comment means.
fn orphan_prose_floor() with Pure = 240

// ─── The effect rosters, read for what anything actually performs ────
//
// An effect is a promise that a handler can intercept an operation. An op
// nobody performs is that promise unkept — either the surface is half
// built, or the callers reached past it, or the whole effect died and its
// handler is still installed.
//
// This exists because the unreferenced read above kept surfacing that
// class and could not name it: its rows are names, so telling an op from
// a fn from a type meant reading each one by hand, three landings running.
// BodyContext (four ops, zero readers, installed in every lowering route),
// Interact (22 ops, 14 never performed), and the Filesystem bypass were
// all found that way. Grouping by the effect that declares them makes the
// shape visible in one read: k/n says at a glance whether a surface is
// whole, half-wired, or dead.
//
// The count is per-op and the same one `unreferenced` uses — reference
// sites outside the declaration's own extent — so a handler ARM does not
// count as a perform, which is exactly right: an arm is the promise's
// other half, not its consumer.
fn effect_report(cells) with !Mutate = fold([], effect_rows_join, cells)

fn effect_rows_join(acc, cell) with !Mutate = acc ++ effect_rows_for(cell)

fn effect_rows_for(cell) with !Mutate = match cell {
  (_h, path, _, decls) => fold([], effect_row_join(path), decls),
}

fn effect_row_join(path: String, acc, d) with !Mutate = match d {
  N(body, sp, _) => match body {
    NStmt(s) => match s {
      EffectDeclStmt(n, _, ops) => push(acc, (path, n, len(ops), count(op_is_performed(sp), ops))),
      _ => acc,
    },
    _ => acc,
  },
}

fn op_is_performed(extent, op) with !Mutate = match op {
  (on, _, _, _) => count(ref_is_outside(extent), graph_refs_at(on)) > 0,
}

// A ref site counts when it has a real span and that span does not fall
// within the declaration's own extent. A spanless site proves nothing about
// where it is, so it never counts as an outside reader.
fn ref_is_outside(extent, site) with !Mutate = {
  let sp = span_of_node_raw(site)
  sp != span_zero() && !span_overlaps(extent, sp)
}

// ─── The decls column's projection — one home, two readers ──────────
//
// decl_handles — the judged DECLARATION handles (fn, value binding,
// handler, type, effect, alias), read from the graph's decls column. A
// speculative judgment's rounds re-note one declaration, so each handle is
// kept at its first sight, read off a word map in one pass; the "decls"
// facet renders them named and located.
fn decl_handles() = {
  let hs = graph_decls_at()
  match hs |> fold(([], wmap_new(len(hs) + 1)), first_sight) {
    (kept, _) => kept,
  }
}

fn first_sight(acc, h) = match acc {
  (kept, seen) => match wmap_get(seen, h) {
    Some(_) => acc,
    None => (push(kept, h), wmap_add(seen, h, ())),
  },
}

// ─── The structural census — count + sites of a shape in the weave ──
//
// The same walk refs_of_name runs (every node once, span-deduped —
// re-parsed judgment generations mint one source site many times), with
// the membership read from the shape. A membership predicate's false arm
// is the read's honest complement over NodeBody, not a fabricated value.
fn census_label(shape) = match shape {
  CsAnonymous => "anonymous fn(s)",
  CsVerb(k) => "{show_pipe_kind(k)} site(s)",
  CsEta => "eta-wrapper(s)",
  CsEffectfulLambda => "effectful lambda(s)",
  CsIterationCostume => "iteration-costume fn(s)",
  CsWildcardZero => "wildcard-zero arm(s)",
  CsFailureMask => "failure-mask site(s)",
  CsPrintInReport => "print-in-report site(s)",
  CsWildcardFabricates => "wildcard-fabricates arm(s)",
  CsUnderscoreRetain => "underscore-retain let(s)",
  CsFlagAsInt => "flag-as-int compare(s)",
  CsParallelArrays => "parallel-arrays site(s)",
  CsEnvFrame => "env-frame site(s)",
  CsVtableRecord => "vtable-record literal(s)",
  CsDefaultParam => "default-valued parameter(s)",
  CsDeclaredRowHof => "fn(s) with a declared row that call a parameter",
  CsAuthoredPositiveRow => "signature(s) authoring a positive row the judgment projects",
  CsRecordPattern => "record pattern(s)",
  CsText(needle) => "string literal(s) containing `{needle}`",
  CsProse(needle) => "comment(s) containing `{needle}`",
  CsProseClass(c) => "comment(s) carrying {prose_class_label(c)}",
}

fn prose_class_label(c) = match c {
  PcCoordinate => "a module coordinate",
  PcPathNoFile => "a path that names no file",
  PcCommit => "a hex commit token",
  PcSpecCitation => "a spec citation",
  PcLifecycle => "lifecycle prose",
  PcArchaeology => "archaeology",
  PcBanner => "a decoration run",
  PcSilencer => "a gate silenced",
}

// The name a question asks a shape by — the one home the query grammar reads
// (census_shape_of) and the roster prints, so a shape is spelled once.
fn census_name(shape) = match shape {
  CsAnonymous => "anonymous",
  CsVerb(k) => show_pipe_kind(k),
  CsEta => "eta",
  CsEffectfulLambda => "effectful-lambda",
  CsIterationCostume => "iteration",
  CsWildcardZero => "wildcard-zero",
  CsFailureMask => "failure-mask",
  CsPrintInReport => "print-in-report",
  CsWildcardFabricates => "wildcard-fabricates",
  CsUnderscoreRetain => "underscore-retain",
  CsFlagAsInt => "flag-as-int",
  CsParallelArrays => "parallel-arrays",
  CsVtableRecord => "vtable-record",
  CsEnvFrame => "env-frame",
  CsDefaultParam => "default-param",
  CsDeclaredRowHof => "declared-row-hof",
  CsAuthoredPositiveRow => "positive-row",
  CsRecordPattern => "record-pattern",
  CsText(needle) => "text {needle}",
  CsProse(needle) => "prose {needle}",
  CsProseClass(c) => "prose-{prose_class_name(c)}",
}

fn prose_class_name(c) = match c {
  PcCoordinate => "coordinate",
  PcPathNoFile => "path",
  PcCommit => "commit",
  PcSpecCitation => "spec",
  PcLifecycle => "lifecycle",
  PcArchaeology => "archaeology",
  PcBanner => "banner",
  PcSilencer => "silencer",
}

fn prose_classes() =
  [PcCoordinate, PcPathNoFile, PcCommit, PcSpecCitation, PcLifecycle, PcArchaeology, PcBanner, PcSilencer]

// THE ROSTER — every shape the census answers without a needle, listable
// (`mentl query <entry> census`). It answered "unknown query" until G2, so
// the only way to learn the shapes was to read this file.
fn census_roster() =
  [CsAnonymous, CsEta, CsEffectfulLambda, CsIterationCostume] ++ drift_roster() ++ [CsDefaultParam, CsDeclaredRowHof, CsAuthoredPositiveRow, CsRecordPattern] ++ map(CsVerb, [PForward, PFanout(FanShare), PFanout(FanDistribute), PTee, PFeedback]) ++ map(CsProseClass, prose_classes())

fn roster_row(shape) = (census_name(shape), census_label(shape), len(census_sites(shape)))

// A shape by the name a question asks it with: the roster read, never a
// second spelling of it.
fn census_shape_named(arg: String) = find(census_named(arg), census_roster())

fn census_named(arg: String, shape) = census_name(shape) == arg

// A LOCATED answer: the label and the (module, span) sites of a question the
// medium answers by place — the shape the board bounds. A question answered
// by a type, a reason or a table has no sites, and the board refuses a bound
// over it rather than counting nothing.
fn located_answer(r) = match r {
  QRCensus(label, sites) => Some((label, sites)),
  QRGhosts(sites) => Some(("cell(s) minted and never judged", sites)),
  QRVerifyDebt(obligations) => Some(("open obligation(s)", map(debt_site, obligations))),
  QRSmt(obligations) => Some(("open obligation(s)", map(debt_site, obligations))),
  QRDecls(names) => Some(("declared name(s)", names |> map({ (_, site) => site }))),
  QRRefs(sites) => Some(("reference(s)", sites)),
  QRType(_, _) => None,
  QRIntent(_) => None,
  QRUnresolved(_) => None,
  QRChain(_) => None,
  QREffects(_) => None,
  QROwnership(_) => None,
  QRWhy(_) => None,
  QRHandlerProvider(_) => None,
  QRFlowLabel(_, _) => None,
  QRCensusRoster(_) => None,
  QRModules(_) => None,
  QRImports(_) => None,
  QRUnreferenced(_) => None,
  QRUnreachable(_) => None,
  QRPerforms(_) => None,
  QROrphanClaims(_) => None,
  QRCost(_, _, _) => None,
  QRWhere(_, _) => None,
  QRError(_) => None,
}

fn debt_site(ob) = match ob {
  (modh, s, _, _) => (module_name_of_handle(modh), s),
}

fn census_sites(shape) with !Mutate = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(next, _) => map({ (m, sp, _h) => (m, sp) }, census_walk(shape, 0, next, make_list(0))),
}

// The weave walk's span is the node's OWN (raw) reason — the parse site.
// span_of_handle CHASES to the union-find root, which conflates a node's
// identity with its type-class representative: a `<~` node's chase lands
// on the continuation-boundary cell (bound with a bare Inferred), answers
// span_zero, and the census skipped every feedback site as synthetic —
// the 2.0 dig's true root, found after the eq itself measured TRUE
// (3 vs 3) on the pair the divergence hypothesis had accused.
fn span_of_node_raw(handle) with !Mutate = span_of_reason(graph_reason_at(handle))

// The dedup key is (MODULE, span), not the span alone. It was the span alone
// while spans were offsets into ONE concatenated weave, where a coordinate was
// globally unique by construction. The per-module parse makes every file start
// at line 1, so two modules genuinely share a line 10 and span-keyed dedup
// silently DROPS the second site — measured the day the parse moved: the verb
// census missed its own fixture's `|>` because a lib module had already
// claimed that line. The pair is unique again for the reason the coordinate
// used to be, and now it says so instead of relying on it.
// A site in a SUPERSEDED generation is not a site of the program: a session
// that re-judged a module holds its older parse below the current one, at
// spans the edit has since moved, so the (module, span) dedup cannot catch it
// (graph_node_current).
fn census_walk(shape, i, n, acc) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  acc
} else {
  if census_matches(shape, i, graph_node_body(i)) && graph_node_current(i) {
    let sp = census_site_at(shape, i)
    // A MODULE'S IDENTITY IS ITS PATH, never the node that represents it —
    // the same law driver_collect_visit's visited set already keys on. The
    // trial and the final each parse, so each mints its OWN NModule node for
    // the same file: keying the dedup on the HANDLE made one site read as two
    // (measured: every census count exactly DOUBLED, types:3748 four times).
    let m = module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(i))
    if sp == span_zero() || site_in(acc, m, sp, 0) {
      census_walk(shape, i + 1, n, acc)
    } else {
      census_walk(shape, i + 1, n, push(acc, (m, sp, i)))
    }
  } else {
    census_walk(shape, i + 1, n, acc)
  }
}

// A site is where the shape stands: the node's own span, except for prose,
// which stands where the comment does.
fn census_site_at(shape, h) with !Mutate = match shape {
  CsProse(_) => graph_comment_span_at(h),
  CsProseClass(_) => graph_comment_span_at(h),
  _ => span_of_node_raw(h),
}

fn site_in(ref acc, ref m: String, ref sp, i) = if i >= len(acc) {
  false
} else {
  match acc[i] {
    (am, asp, _h) => if am == m && asp == sp { true } else { site_in(acc, m, sp, i + 1) },
  }
}

// The annotations are Intent Boundaries carrying the eq proof: without
// them the kinds' `==` floored to pointer i32.eq (the documented
// pointer-eq-on-names class, here on an ADT — PFeedback's two
// construction sites compared unequal while a match on either side
// dispatched PFeedback true; measured [CW] `<~ vs <~` → false). The
// handle rides beside the body: the judgment shapes read the graph at
// the site (the lambda's judged row, the fn's own self-calls), where
// the syntactic shapes read the body alone.
fn census_matches(shape: CensusShape, h, body: NodeBody) with !Mutate = match shape {
  CsAnonymous => match body {
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(params, lbody)) => lambda_is_written_as_a_mint(params, lbody),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsVerb(k) => match body {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(pk, _, _)) => pk == k,
    NExpr(FanoutExpr(_)) => // the N-ary carrier IS the `><` site; the kind
    // stays the census key (Hβ.parser.pcompose-nary)
    k == PFanout(FanDistribute),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsEta => match body {
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(params, lbody)) => lambda_is_eta(params, lbody),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsEffectfulLambda => match body {
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(params, lbody)) => lambda_is_written_as_a_mint(params, lbody) && !lambda_is_eta(params, lbody) && lambda_carries_row(h),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsIterationCostume => match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, _)) => recursion_shape_of(h),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsWildcardZero => // Drift mode 10's shape (the 5.6 absorption's first instance —
  // the detector moves from bash grep into the weave): a match arm
  // whose pattern is the wildcard and whose body is the literal 0 —
  // the surrender-fallback silhouette. The census COUNTS the shape;
  // whether a given site is the masked-case drift or a true total
  // answer (a non-fn's arity IS zero) stays the reader's judgment,
  // exactly as the bash mode's suppression comment encoded it.
  match body {
    NExpr(MatchExpr(_, arms)) => any_wildcard_zero(arms, 0, len(arms)),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsFailureMask => // Drift mode 13's shape (the second absorption): `expr || true`
  // — a failure masked into unconditional success. The census counts
  // the silhouette; a deliberate specimen carries its reader-facing
  // reason exactly as the wildcard-zero convention set.
  match body {
    NExpr(BinOpExpr(op, _, r)) => match op {
      BOr => match r {
        N(rb, _, _) => match rb {
          NExpr(LitBool(v)) => v,
          _ => false,
        },
      },
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  CsPrintInReport => // Drift mode 16's shape (the third absorption): a `report(...)`
  // call whose argument subtree calls the print family — the class
  // that corrupts WAT stdout when diagnostics render mid-emit (§9's
  // bug list). The subtree search walks the weave's own tree edge
  // (body_child_handles), fuel-bounded by the arg tree's real depth.
  match body {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, args)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(callee)) {
      NExpr(VarRef(cn)) => if cn == "report" { any_subtree_prints(args, 0, len(args)) } else { false },
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  CsWildcardFabricates => // Drift mode 10's TYPED fabrications (the fourth absorption):
  // a wildcard arm whose body mints a Scheme (Forall), a fresh type
  // var (TVar), the string "Pure", or the empty string — the four
  // values a missing case silently absorbs a new variant into. The
  // zero literal is CsWildcardZero's own shape; these are its typed
  // siblings, name-keyed on the minting ctor exactly as
  // CsPrintInReport keys on the print family.
  match body {
    NExpr(MatchExpr(_, arms)) => any_wildcard_fabricates(arms, 0, len(arms)),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsUnderscoreRetain => // Drift mode 15's shape (the fifth absorption): a let whose
  // binder KEEPS a leading underscore — an unused value renamed
  // instead of deleted. A bare `_` is PWild at parse, so any PVar
  // opening with the underscore is the retained costume; deletion,
  // not decoration, is the fix the mode has always named.
  match body {
    NStmt(LetStmt(p, _, _)) => match p {
      PVar(name) => starts_with(name, "_"),
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  CsFlagAsInt => // Drift mode 8's shape (the sixth absorption): a flag compared
  // as an integer — `mode == 0`, `token_kind == 2`, `scheme_kind == 1`
  // — the int-coded state an ADT is begging to replace. Name-keyed on
  // the three flag identifiers the mode always policed, exactly as
  // CsPrintInReport keys on the print family; the literal side is the
  // int, either operand order.
  match body {
    NExpr(BinOpExpr(op, l, r)) => match op {
      BEq => flag_int_compare(l, r) || flag_int_compare(r, l),
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  CsParallelArrays => // Drift mode 7's shape (the seventh absorption): parallel
  // arrays where one record was native, in its TWO faces. The let-tuple
  // face: a destructuring bind of a tuple whose FIRST TWO binders both
  // carry the _h suffix (the surface let desugars at parse into the
  // one-arm match — desugar_block — so the weave shape covers both
  // spellings). The PARAM-ADJACENCY face (the mode's second bash row,
  // absorbed): a fn whose parameter list carries an adjacent `x, x_h`
  // pair — the paired arrays threaded as paired arguments; structural
  // over ANY name where the regex saw four literals. The pair anchor
  // is the mode's own settled note: ctor payloads legitimately carry
  // the handle convention; the paired bind/params convict.
  match body {
    NExpr(MatchExpr(_, arms)) => if len(arms) == 1 {
      match list_index(arms, 0) {
        (p, _) => match p {
          PTuple(elems) => paired_handle_binders(elems),
          _ => false,
        },
      }
    } else {
      false
    },
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, params, _, _, _)) => adjacent_handle_params(params |> map(param_name)),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsVtableRecord => // Drift mode 1's shape (the eighth absorption): a record
  // literal with a field NAMED like a dispatch slot (fn_* / handler_*)
  // holding a lambda — Mentl drawn as Rust. The name key is the
  // mode's own anchor: a record of closures with HONEST field names
  // (the unified each's leaf record) is a deliberate design, so only
  // the machinery-named slot convicts. Structural where the regex saw
  // only the retired lambda spellings; the nominal-record costume
  // counts too.
  match body {
    NExpr(MakeRecordExpr(flds)) => any_vtable_field(flds, 0, len(flds)),
    NExpr(NamedRecordExpr(_, flds)) => any_vtable_field(flds, 0, len(flds)),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsEnvFrame => // Drift mode 2's shape (the tenth absorption): the
  // scope-as-frame-stack family — parent_env / env_stack / push_frame /
  // pop_frame / env_chain — declared, called, or bound. Env lookup is
  // an effect op (the bare `env_lookup(name)` call); a parent-pointer
  // frame walk re-derives what the env edge already connects. The name
  // key is the mode's own anchor, exactly as CsFlagAsInt and
  // CsPrintInReport key on their families.
  match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => env_frame_name(name),
    NExpr(VarRef(name)) => env_frame_name(name),
    NStmt(LetStmt(p, _, _)) => match p {
      PVar(name) => env_frame_name(name),
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  CsDefaultParam => // The declared surface read from the carrier that already holds it:
  // a default is TParam slot 4, an Option(Node) the parser fills at the
  // declaration, so this is a chase to a fact the graph proves and never
  // a re-derivation from source text. SYNTAX makes the slot load-bearing
  // rather than decorative — the default is a callee-scoped fill, so its
  // row is the callee's row, which is why the crown pins it as a carrier
  // (leak-default-latent / sound-default-transport).
  match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, params, _, _, _)) => any(
      (p) => match param_default(p) {
        Some(_) => true,
        None => false,
      },
      params
    ),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsDeclaredRowHof => // The population `Hβ.infer.declared-row-vacuous-against-a-free-body-row`
  // needs priced before its fix is built: a fn that DECLARES a row and
  // CALLS one of its own parameters. Today the declaration is vacuous
  // against the param's free row var — `fn run(f) with !E = f()` accepts
  // an effectful callback and runs — and the fix makes the declaration a
  // real constraint on that var, so every fn counted here is a site whose
  // judgment changes. Both facts are read where the graph holds them: the
  // signed clauses are FnStmt's own field, and the call is the weave's
  // tree edge walked to the callee's VarRef.
  match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, params, _, effs, fbody)) => if len(effs) == 0 {
      false
    } else {
      subtree_calls_param(node_handle(fbody), map(param_name, params), 64)
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  CsAuthoredPositiveRow => // The declared clauses are `FnStmt`'s own field — the row is read where
  // the signature holds it, never re-parsed from the page. A term is
  // (name, negated, connective): a bare un-negated name is the shape; a
  // negation, an instance pin and Pure are decisions and are not.
  match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, effs, _)) => any(term_is_bare_positive, effs),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsRecordPattern => // Both spellings of one shape, read where the pattern lives: a
  // let-binding's own pattern and a match arm's. The emit reads a record
  // pattern's field offsets from the receiver's whole field set under the
  // twin being emitted, so every site counted here is a structural read of
  // its receiver's row.
  match body {
    NStmt(LetStmt(p, _, _)) => match p {
      PRecord(_, _) => true,
      _ => false,
    },
    NExpr(MatchExpr(_, arms)) => any_record_arm(arms, 0, len(arms)),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsText(needle) => match body {
    NExpr(LitString(s)) => str_contains(s, needle),
    _ => false,
  },
  CsProse(needle) => prose_holds(graph_comment_at(h), needle),
  CsProseClass(c) => prose_class_holds(c, graph_comment_at(h)),
}

// A node with no comment holds no prose, so an empty needle lists every
// comment and never every node.
fn prose_holds(t: String, needle: String) =
  byte_len(t) > 0 && (byte_len(needle) == 0 || holds_token(t, needle))

// THE TOKEN-BOUNDED READ: some occurrence of the needle whose word-byte ends
// meet non-word bytes. The comment split at the needle puts each occurrence
// between two neighbouring parts, so the boundary test reads the part before
// it and the part after it — no index walked.
fn holds_token(t: String, needle: String) = {
  let parts = split(needle, t)
  zip(parts, rest(parts)) |> any(bounded_between(needle))
}

fn bounded_between(needle: String, pair) = match pair {
  (before, after) => start_bounded(needle, before) && end_bounded(needle, after),
}

fn start_bounded(needle: String, before: String) =
  !is_alnum(byte_at(needle, 0)) || byte_len(before) == 0 || !is_alnum(byte_at(before, byte_len(before) - 1))

fn end_bounded(needle: String, after: String) =
  !is_alnum(byte_at(needle, byte_len(needle) - 1)) || byte_len(after) == 0 || !is_alnum(byte_at(after, 0))

// A class's read of one comment.
fn prose_class_holds(c, t: String) = byte_len(t) > 0 && match c {
  PcCoordinate => holds_followed_by_digit(t, ".mn:"),
  PcPathNoFile => comment_tokens(t) |> any(names_no_file),
  PcCommit => comment_tokens(t) |> any(is_commit_token),
  PcSpecCitation => holds_followed_by_digit(t, "spec "),
  PcLifecycle => any(holds_token(t), ["no longer", "previously", "legacy"]) || holds_followed_by_digit(t, "Until 20"),
  PcArchaeology => any(holds_token(t), ["walkthrough", "walkthroughs", "seed's", "DELETED"]),
  PcBanner => decoration_run(t) >= 3,
  PcSilencer => str_contains(t, "drift-audit: ignore"),
}

// An occurrence, start-bounded, that a digit follows: a coordinate's line, a
// spec's number, a year.
fn holds_followed_by_digit(t: String, needle: String) = {
  let parts = split(needle, t)
  zip(parts, rest(parts)) |> any(digit_follows(needle))
}

fn digit_follows(needle: String, pair) = match pair {
  (before, after) => start_bounded(needle, before) && byte_len(after) > 0 && is_digit(byte_at(after, 0)),
}

// The comment's whitespace-separated words, each trimmed of the punctuation
// prose wraps around a token — a backtick, a paren, a trailing comma.
fn comment_tokens(t: String) = t |> words |> map(token_core)

fn token_core(w: String) = if byte_len(w) == 0 {
  w
} else if !token_byte(byte_at(w, 0)) {
  token_core(str_slice(w, 1, byte_len(w)))
} else if !token_byte(byte_at(w, byte_len(w) - 1)) {
  token_core(str_slice(w, 0, byte_len(w) - 1))
} else {
  w
}

fn token_byte(b) with Pure = is_alnum(b)

// A commit token: seven to forty lowercase hex bytes, with a digit and a
// letter both — a count is all digits and a word is all letters.
fn is_commit_token(w: String) =
  byte_len(w) >= 7 && byte_len(w) <= 40 && all(is_hex_lower, w) && any(is_digit, w) && any(is_hex_letter, w)

fn is_hex_lower(b) with Pure = is_digit(b) || is_hex_letter(b)

fn is_hex_letter(b) with Pure = b >= 97 && b <= 102

// A repository path is a token rooted in one of the tree's own directories
// and ending in a file extension; it names no file when the filesystem the
// question runs over holds none at it. A glob or a template (a placeholder in
// braces or angle brackets) names a pattern, never a file.
fn names_no_file(w: String) =
  repo_rooted(w) && has_extension(w) && !names_a_pattern(w) && !fs_exists(w)

fn names_a_pattern(w: String) = any(str_contains(w), ["*", "\{", "<", ">"])

fn repo_rooted(w: String) =
  any(starts_with(w), ["src/", "lib/", "tools/", "tests/", "docs/", "ide/", "examples/", "boot/"])

fn has_extension(w: String) = any(
  ends_with(w),
  [".mn", ".sh", ".md", ".py", ".js", ".mjs", ".tsv", ".txt", ".json", ".wasm", ".wat", ".html", ".css"]
)

// The longest run of decoration glyphs in a comment: box-drawing (U+2500 to
// U+257F, three bytes each, lead 0xE2 then 0x94 or 0x95) or an equals sign.
// The fold carries (run, best, where the byte stands inside a glyph).
fn decoration_run(t: String) = match fold((0, 0, GlyphBetween), decoration_step, t) {
  (_, best, _) => best,
}

// Where a byte stands relative to a three-byte box-drawing glyph.
type GlyphPhase = GlyphBetween | GlyphAfterLead | GlyphAfterBlock

fn decoration_step(state, b) = match state {
  (run, best, phase) => match phase {
    GlyphAfterLead => if b == 148 || b == 149 { (run, best, GlyphAfterBlock) } else { (0, best, GlyphBetween) },
    GlyphAfterBlock => (run + 1, max(best, run + 1), GlyphBetween),
    GlyphBetween => if b == 226 {
      (run, best, GlyphAfterLead)
    } else if b == 61 {
      (run + 1, max(best, run + 1), GlyphBetween)
    } else {
      (0, best, GlyphBetween)
    },
  },
}

// The match half of CsRecordPattern, walked by index the way this file's
// sibling arm-walkers are.
fn any_record_arm(arms, i, n) = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  match list_index(arms, i) {
    (p, _) => match p {
      PRecord(_, _) => true,
      _ => any_record_arm(arms, i + 1, n),
    },
  }
}

// Mode 2's family membership — the five frame-stack names the bash
// regex policed, now read from the weave.
fn env_frame_name(name) with Pure =
  name == "parent_env" || name == "env_stack" || name == "push_frame" || name == "pop_frame" || name == "env_chain"

fn any_vtable_field(flds, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  match list_index(flds, i) {
    (fname, fval) => if starts_with(fname, "fn_") || starts_with(fname, "handler_") {
      match graph_node_body(node_handle(fval)) {
        NExpr(LambdaExpr(_, _)) => true,
        _ => any_vtable_field(flds, i + 1, n),
      }
    } else {
      any_vtable_field(flds, i + 1, n)
    },
  }
}

fn paired_handle_binders(elems) = if len(elems) < 2 {
  false
} else {
  match (list_index(elems, 0), list_index(elems, 1)) {
    (PVar(a), PVar(b)) => ends_with(a, "_h") && ends_with(b, "_h"),
    _ => false,
  }
}

// The param face's pairwise walk: any adjacent (a, b) where b is
// exactly a ++ "_h" — the paired-array threading the bash regex saw
// only for four literal names. Structural recursion over the list's
// own shape; `rest` keeps the slice O(1) per step.
fn adjacent_handle_params(names) = match names {
  [a, b, ..._] => if b == a ++ "_h" { true } else { adjacent_handle_params(rest(names)) },
  _ => false,
}

fn flag_int_compare(a, b) = match graph_node_body(node_handle(a)) {
  NExpr(VarRef(n)) => if n == "mode" || n == "token_kind" || n == "scheme_kind" {
    match graph_node_body(node_handle(b)) {
      NExpr(LitInt(_)) => true,
      _ => false,
    }
  } else {
    false
  },
  _ => false,
}

fn any_subtree_prints(nodes, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  if subtree_prints(node_handle(list_index(nodes, i)), 64) {
    true
  } else {
    any_subtree_prints(nodes, i + 1, n)
  }
}

fn subtree_prints(h, fuel) with !Mutate = if fuel <= 0 {
  false
} else {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = match body {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(callee)) {
      NExpr(VarRef(cn)) => cn == "println" || cn == "print" || cn == "print_string",
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  }
  if here { true } else { subtree_prints_children(body_child_handles(body), fuel - 1) }
}

fn subtree_prints_children(hs, fuel) with !Mutate = match hs {
  [] => false,
  [ch, ...rest] => if subtree_prints(ch, fuel) { true } else { subtree_prints_children(rest, fuel) },
}

// Does this subtree CALL one of the names in `pnames`? The callee's own
// VarRef is the read — a param mentioned as an argument is transport and
// does not constrain anything, only a call puts the param's row into the
// enclosing body's row.
fn subtree_calls_param(h, pnames, fuel) with !Mutate = if fuel <= 0 {
  false
} else {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = match body {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(callee)) {
      NExpr(VarRef(cn)) => set_contains(pnames, cn),
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  }
  if here { true } else { subtree_calls_param_children(body_child_handles(body), pnames, fuel - 1) }
}

fn subtree_calls_param_children(hs, pnames, fuel) with !Mutate = match hs {
  [] => false,
  [ch, ...rest] => if subtree_calls_param(ch, pnames, fuel) {
    true
  } else {
    subtree_calls_param_children(rest, pnames, fuel)
  },
}

// The per-fn drift read (the audit's absorbed-mode tier): the absorbed
// drift shapes counted within ONE fn's body subtree — the same
// membership the whole-weave census runs, scoped by the tree edge, so
// the audit names the costume where it stands and the census answers
// the corpus question. The ROSTER is the one home for membership: a
// newly absorbed mode joins by one entry here (the widening
// count-tuple this replaced was the per-site family at seven arms —
// the uniform pass, applied to the tier's own construction).
fn drift_roster() =
  [CsWildcardZero, CsFailureMask, CsPrintInReport, CsWildcardFabricates, CsUnderscoreRetain, CsFlagAsInt, CsParallelArrays, CsVtableRecord, CsEnvFrame]

fn drift_shapes_of(fn_handle) with !Mutate = {
  // The walk starts at the FN NODE, not its body: a shape can live ON
  // the FnStmt itself (the param-adjacency face), and
  // body_child_handles already descends into the body — one uniform
  // walk, no per-kind carve-out.
  let roster = drift_roster()
  zip(roster, subtree_drift_counts(fn_handle, roster, map((_) => 0, roster), 128))
}

fn subtree_drift_counts(h, roster, counts, fuel) with !Mutate = if fuel <= 0 {
  counts
} else {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let counts2 = roster_bump(roster, counts, 0, len(roster), h, body)
  subtree_drift_children(body_child_handles(body), roster, counts2, fuel - 1)
}

fn roster_bump(roster, counts, i, n, h, body) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  counts
} else {
  let c2 = if census_matches(list_index(roster, i), h, body) {
    list_set(counts, i, list_index(counts, i) + 1)
  } else {
    counts
  }
  roster_bump(roster, c2, i + 1, n, h, body)
}

fn subtree_drift_children(hs, roster, counts, fuel) with !Mutate = match hs {
  [] => counts,
  [ch, ...rest] => subtree_drift_children(rest, roster, subtree_drift_counts(ch, roster, counts, fuel), fuel),
}

fn any_wildcard_zero(arms, i, n) = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  match list_index(arms, i) {
    (p, b) => match p {
      PWild => match b {
        N(nb, _, _) => match nb {
          NExpr(LitInt(0)) => true,
          _ => any_wildcard_zero(arms, i + 1, n),
        },
      },
      _ => any_wildcard_zero(arms, i + 1, n),
    },
  }
}

fn any_wildcard_fabricates(arms, i, n) = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  match list_index(arms, i) {
    (p, b) => match p {
      PWild => if wildcard_body_fabricates(b) { true } else { any_wildcard_fabricates(arms, i + 1, n) },
      _ => any_wildcard_fabricates(arms, i + 1, n),
    },
  }
}

fn wildcard_body_fabricates(b) = match b {
  N(nb, _, _) => match nb {
    NExpr(LitString(s)) => // drift-audit: ignore — the census DETECTOR reading the drifted literal from the weave, not an effect-name dispatch
    s == "" || s == "Pure",
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(callee)) {
      NExpr(VarRef(cn)) => cn == "Forall" || cn == "TVar",
      _ => false,
    },
    _ => false,
  },
}

// The one total child projection over NodeBody — `body_child_handles` and
// its Expr/Stmt halves — lives beside `node_handle` in graph.mn (moved
// 2026-09-27 so lower's race rule can walk a handler's arms with the same
// edge the census walks; every tree walk here reads it, never a copy).
//
// ── the tier detectors — the census IS their home ────────────────────
//
// The audit tiers (`anonymity_shape_of` per fn, below; pipeline.mn's
// renderer) READ these; the census counts them over the whole weave.
// One detector, two projections — the per-fn narration and the
// count+sites answer — never two classifiers (PLAN §11 Phase 2.5).
//
// THE FAMILY HAS ONE HOME AS OF 2026-09-21, and this comment named the
// split it was living in: two of the audit's four shape reads
// (`pipe_shape_of`, `anonymity_shape_of` with their ten helpers) lived in
// src/oracle.mn, a module whose entire subject was the superseded
// ranked-queue oracle, and whose other 25 decls were unreachable. They are
// below, beside `drift_shapes_of` and `recursion_shape_of`, and oracle.mn is
// deleted.
//
// ITERATION IS TOPOLOGY (Morgan's interrogation, 2026-07-30 — the law
// SYNTAX declared at birth: no loop keywords; iteration is |> stages,
// <~ cycles, Iterate handlers). A self-call that threads an
// INCREMENTED INDEX over the fn's own parameter is the imperative loop
// written in recursion's costume — name-keyed re-entry where a derived
// fold/each/iterate serves. The read is structural over the one total
// child projection: a CallExpr of the fn's OWN name with any argument
// `p + 1` / `1 + p` over a parameter — the index-threading signature
// (a structural fold's self-call recurses on CHILDREN, never on an
// incremented counter, so derived-fold-shaped recursion never fires).
fn recursion_shape_of(fn_handle) = match graph_node_body(fn_handle) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(name, params, _, _, body)) => self_call_increments(node_handle(body), name, params |> map(param_name)),
  _ => false,
}

// ── the verb-shape read — the topology the bindings already draw ─────
//
// SYNTAX ⌖: `|>` is NEVER optional on a chain — `y |> f |> g` over
// `let x = f(y); g(x)`. The verb is a PROJECTION of use-count (the
// same edge own/ref grades from): a let-bound name consumed EXACTLY
// ONCE, by exactly the FOLLOWING statement, is a `|>` stage written as
// a binding; a name used twice is `<|` territory and never fires; a
// single step is the law's own exception (the pipe wins on CHAINS).
// Counting walks the one total child projection — never a text scan.
//
// body_is_var — is this body exactly a VarRef of `name`? The counting
// walk's leaf test as its own projection (a non-VarRef body is simply
// not the name — the false arm is the predicate's definition).
fn body_is_var(body, name: String) with Pure = match body {
  NExpr(VarRef(n)) => n == name,
  _ => false,
}

// count_var_uses — VarRef occurrences of `name` in the subtree at h.
// An inner rebinding shadows and over-counts toward "more than one
// use", which only SUPPRESSES an invite — conservative by construction.
fn count_var_uses(h, name: String) = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = if body_is_var(body, name) { 1 } else { 0 }
  here + (body_child_handles(body) |> map((c) => count_var_uses(c, name)) |> fold(0, (a, x) => a + x))
}

// chain_scan — walk a block's statement spine (stmts ++ final): a
// LetStmt(PVar) whose name has exactly one use, inside exactly the
// next node, extends the current run; anything else flushes it. Runs
// of length >= 2 are `|>` invites, reported as step counts.
// The spine's links, then the runs — two stages, neither threading an
// index through a self-call. A LINK is per-position (does stmt i feed
// stmt i+1 exactly once?); the runs are a fold over those links
// carrying (run, acc), flushing a run of >= 2 as one `|>` invite.
fn chain_scan(nodes) = match chain_links(nodes) |> fold(
  (0, []),
  (state, link) => match state {
    (run, acc) => if link { (run + 1, acc) } else { (0, keep_run(run, acc)) },
  }
) {
  (run, acc) => keep_run(run, acc),
}

// A run of two or more bindings IS the chain the pipe draws; one step is
// the law's own exception.
fn keep_run(run, acc) = if run >= 2 { acc ++ [run] } else { acc }

fn chain_links(nodes) = if len(nodes) < 2 {
  []
} else {
  range(0, len(nodes) - 1) |> map((i) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(nodes[i])) {
    NStmt(LetStmt(PVar(name), _, _)) => let_feeds_next(name, nodes, i),
    _ => false,
  })
}

fn let_feeds_next(name: String, nodes, i) =
  count_var_uses(node_handle(nodes[i + 1]), name) == 1 && rest_uses(name, nodes, i + 2) == 0

// The suffix's total use-count — a `|>` chain over the tail, not an
// index threaded through a self-call (iteration is topology; this is
// the tier's own neighbour, convicted by the tier it sits four lines
// above).
fn rest_uses(name: String, nodes, j) =
  nodes |> drop(j) |> map((n) => count_var_uses(node_handle(n), name)) |> sum

// pipe_shape_of — the verb-shape read over a fn's body spine, the
// audit tier's entry (build_audit_report reads it per fn).
fn pipe_shape_of(fn_handle) = match graph_node_body(fn_handle) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, body)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(body)) {
    NExpr(BlockExpr(stmts, fin)) => chain_scan(stmts ++ [fin]),
    _ => [],
  },
  _ => [],
}

// ── the anonymity read — the named stage in hiding ───────────────────
//
// INTENT IS LOSSLESS (PLAN §0; the stamp Hβ.audit.anonymity-tier): an
// anonymous fn that already HAS a name (the eta-wrapper — its body is
// exactly a named call over its own params, so the name exists and the
// lambda hides it) or that CARRIES a row (a non-Pure lambda — the
// effects deserve a decl home with a `with` clause) is intent
// discarded at the site that knew it. The pure-local
// immediately-consumed lambda is the vocabulary and stays silent —
// `map((x) => x + 1, xs)` never narrates, whatever param it lands on.
// The escape class is DEP `Hβ.infer.use-profile` (band N): until the
// escape-bit lands, the read speaks the two classes it can prove.
fn anonymity_shape_of(fn_handle) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(fn_handle) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, body)) => lambda_shapes(node_handle(body), (0, 0)),
  _ => (0, 0),
}

fn lambda_shapes(h, acc) with !Mutate = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = match body {
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(params, lbody)) => classify_lambda(h, params, lbody, acc),
    _ => acc,
  }
  body_child_handles(body) |> fold(here, (a, c) => lambda_shapes(c, a))
}

fn classify_lambda(h, params, lbody, acc) with !Mutate = match acc {
  (eta, rowed) => if lambda_is_eta(params, lbody) {
    (eta + 1, rowed)
  } else if lambda_carries_row(h) {
    (eta, rowed + 1)
  } else {
    acc
  },
}

fn self_call_increments(h, name: String, pnames) = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = match body {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, args)) => callee_names(callee, name) && (args |> any((a) => arg_is_increment(a, pnames))),
    _ => false,
  }
  here || (body_child_handles(body) |> any((c) => self_call_increments(c, name, pnames)))
}

fn callee_names(callee, name: String) = match graph_node_body(node_handle(callee)) {
  NExpr(VarRef(n)) => n == name,
  _ => false,
}

fn arg_is_increment(arg, pnames) = match graph_node_body(node_handle(arg)) {
  NExpr(BinOpExpr(BAdd, l, r)) => param_plus_one(l, r, pnames) || param_plus_one(r, l, pnames),
  _ => false,
}

fn param_plus_one(a, b, pnames) = match graph_node_body(node_handle(a)) {
  NExpr(VarRef(n)) => string_in_list(pnames, n) && match graph_node_body(node_handle(b)) {
    NExpr(LitInt(1)) => true,
    _ => false,
  },
  _ => false,
}

// THE ANONYMITY CENSUS COUNTS A REFERENCE WRITTEN AS A MINT, and this
// predicate is where that sentence becomes a measurement. The medium has
// two operations on a function value (PLAN §2): REFERENCE an existing one
// with a hole, or MINT one with an arm list. A lambda whose whole body is
// pattern dispatch is the SECOND — no reference-with-a-hole expresses it,
// so convicting it made the count a number that could not reach zero, and
// a ratchet on an unreachable floor measures nothing.
//
// It is also what the page now says. The arm-list literal (SYNTAX
// §«Function literals») renders with no param list at all, so counting
// `{ Leaf => 3, Node(n) => n }` as a LAMBDA would be the census reporting
// a form the source does not contain — the doc-layer Carried-Truth
// violation, one layer down.
fn lambda_is_written_as_a_mint(params, lbody) = match arm_list_of(params, lbody) {
  Some(_) => false,
  None => true,
}

// The eta test reads the body's OWN shape: exactly CallExpr(VarRef(g),
// args) with g resolving OUTSIDE the params (a param-named callee has
// no site-independent name to pass) and args ≡ the params in order.
fn lambda_is_eta(params, lbody) = match graph_node_body(node_handle(lbody)) {
  NExpr(CallExpr(callee, args)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(callee)) {
    NExpr(VarRef(g)) => {
      let pnames = params |> map(param_name)
      !string_in_list(pnames, g) && args_mirror_params(args, pnames)
    },
    _ => false,
  },
  _ => false,
}

fn args_mirror_params(args, pnames) =
  len(args) == len(pnames) && (range(0, len(args)) |> all((i) => arg_is_var(args[i], pnames[i])))

fn arg_is_var(a, n: String) = match graph_node_body(node_handle(a)) {
  NExpr(VarRef(v)) => v == n,
  _ => false,
}

// A lambda that PERFORMS: its judged TFun row resolves to present
// names (resolve_row follows bound row edges, so effects living behind
// an EtOpen var are read, not missed). Nothing present — including an
// open polymorphic tail at a HOF boundary — stays silent: pure
// vocabulary never narrates.
fn lambda_carries_row(h) with !Mutate = match graph_chase(h) {
  GNode(NBound(TFun(_, _, row)), _) => match resolve_row(row) {
    EfRow(present, _, _) => len(present) > 0,
  },
  _ => false,
}

// ── the where projection — derived badges (Hβ.cli.where-verb) ────────
//
// Output, never input: a value's width, an op's resume cardinality, and a
// function's head, parameters, return, installs and fanout sites. Each
// badge is a live read of a fact the graph already proves. A width is the
// repr its type projects, pinned when an authored `repr` decided it
// anywhere along the type's chain; the discipline rides the op's
// EffectOpScheme; an install's class and a site's schedule read the one
// roster the judgment and the lowering read (`projection_of_effects`); a
// site's width is the branch edges its boundary holds. A site's schedule
// is its own frame's — a lambda or a nested fn resets the chain, the frame
// fence's static mirror — and a caller's install reaches it only along a
// demand, which `demanded_vias` reads by the emit's own rule.
//
// A name may name several type-level declarations at once — lib/dsp
// declares a type and an effect both named Sample — so the answer is every
// one of them, each at its address; a name no type-level declaration
// claims answers as a value or a binder.
fn where_badges(name: String, scope: String) with !Mutate = match graph_decls_at() |> filter(decl_is_named(name, ??)) |> flat_map(type_level_lines) {
  [] => value_where(name, scope),
  lines => QRWhere(lines, []),
}

// A type-level declaration's answer, empty for any other: a type's or an
// effect's head at its address, then one line per constructor or op; an
// alias is the line the formatter writes for it, at its address; a handler
// is the effects its arms answer, at its address (the install's badge,
// `tee_line`, read at the declaration).
fn type_level_lines(h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(s) => match s {
    TypeDefStmt(name, variants) => named_lines(flat_map(variant_tys, variants), "type {name}{decl_at(h)}", variant_badge, variants),
    EffectDeclStmt(name, params, ops) => named_lines(
      map(param_ty, params) ++ flat_map(op_tys, ops),
      "effect {name}{effect_params_text(params)}{decl_at(h)}",
      op_badge,
      ops
    ),
    RefineStmt(_, _, _) => ["{render_stmt_tokens(s)}{decl_at(h)}"],
    AliasStmt(_, _) => ["{render_stmt_tokens(s)}{decl_at(h)}"],
    RowAliasStmt(_, _) => ["{render_stmt_tokens(s)}{decl_at(h)}"],
    HandlerDeclStmt(name, _, _, _, arms, _) => [handler_line(name, arms, h)],
    FnStmt(_, _, _, _, _) => [],
    LetStmt(_, _, _) => [],
    ExprStmt(_) => [],
    ImportStmt(_) => [],
  },
  _ => [],
}

// A type's or an effect's lines: the head, then one line per constructor
// or op, its type variables named as a developer writes them (inside a
// checkpoint, as a function's are).
fn named_lines(tys, head: String, line, items) with !Mutate = {
  let cp = graph_push_checkpoint()
  name_type_vars(tys)
  let lines = [head] ++ map(line, items)
  graph_rollback(cp)
  lines
}

fn variant_tys(v) = match v {
  (_, fields, _) => fields,
}

fn op_tys(op) = match op {
  (_, params, ret, _) => push(map(param_ty, params), ret),
}

// The declaration a global name names: its one module-level declaration,
// read off the decls column, which holds every judged declaration — a
// block's `let`s and nested fns beside a module's own. A module-level name is
// unique in the link (`E_DuplicateFnName`), so the first is the one.
fn module_decl(name: String) with !Mutate = find(module_decl_named(name, ??), graph_decls_at())

fn module_decl_named(name: String, h) = decl_is_named(name, h) && at_module_level(h)

fn decl_is_named(name: String, h) = body_decl_name(graph_node_body(h)) == name

// Where a declaration stands: the module its node registered under and the
// line the parser wrote.
fn decl_at(h) with !Mutate = match parse_span_of(h) {
  Span(sl, _, _, _) => "  at {module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(h))}:{int_to_str(sl)}",
}

fn variant_badge(v) with !Mutate = match v {
  (ctor, fields, _) => if len(fields) == 0 { "  {ctor}" } else { "  {ctor}({join(", ", map(show_type, fields))})" },
}

fn op_badge(op) with !Mutate = match op {
  (name, params, ret, _) => "  {name}({join(", ", map(op_param_text, params))}) -> {show_type(ret)}",
}

fn op_param_text(p) with !Mutate = "{param_name(p)}: {show_type(param_ty(p))}"

fn effect_params_text(params) with !Mutate =
  if len(params) == 0 { "" } else { "({join(", ", map(op_param_text, params))})" }

fn value_where(name: String, scope: String) with !Mutate = match env_lookup(name) {
  None => binder_badges(name, scope),
  Some(entry) => match entry {
    (scheme, _, kind) => match kind {
      EffectOpScheme(ename, _, _, disc) => QRWhere(
        ["{name} : {ename} op — resume {op_resume_ty(scheme)} {disc_glyph(disc)} answer{effect_address(ename)}"],
        []
      ),
      _ => {
        let ty = judgment_ty(scheme)
        match chase_type_deep(ty) {
          TFun(params, ret, row) => decl_badges(name, params, ret, row),
          _ => QRWhere([value_badge(name, ty)], []),
        }
      },
    },
  },
}

// A function's badges: its head with the row inference projected (A4 —
// the signature carries only decisions, so the row is read here), each
// parameter's width, its return's, every install in its body with what
// the handler answers, and every fanout site with its schedule. A
// function value with no declaration body (a module let holding a
// closure, a constructor) answers its head, parameters and return.
fn decl_badges(name: String, params, ret, row) with !Mutate = {
  let decl = module_decl(name)
  let body = decl_body(decl)
  let cp = graph_push_checkpoint()
  name_type_vars([TFun(params, ret, row)])
  let lines =
    [decl_head(name, params, row) ++ decl_address(decl)] ++ map(param_badge, params) ++ ["-> {value_type(ret)} @ {width_badge(ret)}"]
  let tees = flat_map(tee_lines, body)
  graph_rollback(cp)
  QRWhere(lines ++ tees, body |> flat_map(fanout_sites(??, [])) |> demand_onto(name))
}

// THE VARIABLES A DEVELOPER WOULD WRITE. A type variable still free renders
// as its handle (`t9538@e599`), the forensic projection's "unresolved at
// epoch e"; a badge is not forensic, and a variable a declaration
// quantifies is generic, not unresolved. Inside a checkpoint each free type
// variable is bound to the name a developer would write — the one it was
// declared with (`a` in `f: a -> b`), else the first letter not taken —
// so the one renderer shows names, and the caller's rollback leaves the
// graph as the projection found it. A raw bind: no judgment reads it, and
// no demand on the cell is consulted. A row variable keeps its handle
// (`Hβ.voice.free-variables-render-as-handles`).
fn name_type_vars(tys) with !Mutate = {
  let vars = tys |> flat_map(free_type_vars) |> unique
  let picked = fold([], pick_name(flat_map(declared_name, vars), ??, ??), vars)
  vars |> enumerate |> each(bind_picked(picked, ??))
}

fn free_type_vars(ty) with !Mutate = ty |> chase_deep |> free_in_ty |> filter(is_type_cell)

fn is_type_cell(v) with !Mutate = match graph_chase(v) {
  GNode(NFree(_), _) => true,
  _ => false,
}

// The name a type parameter was written with: its cell is minted with the
// authored lowercase name as its reason (`quantify_ctor_ty`).
fn declared_name(v) with !Mutate = match graph_reason_at(v) {
  Declared(n) => if is_type_param_name(n) { [n] } else { [] },
  _ => [],
}

fn is_type_param_name(n: String) =
  byte_len(n) > 0 && first_char_code(n) >= 97 && first_char_code(n) <= 122

// A variable's name in first-appearance order: its declared one, else the
// first letter neither declared by another variable nor picked already.
fn pick_name(declared, picked, v) with !Mutate = match declared_name(v) {
  [d] => push(picked, d),
  _ => push(picked, first_untaken(declared ++ picked, 0)),
}

fn bind_picked(picked, iv) with !Mutate = match iv {
  (i, v) => graph_bind(v, TName(picked[i], []), Inferred("the name a developer would write")),
}

// The letters a to z, then t26, t27 and onward — the first not already
// taken.
fn first_untaken(taken, i) = {
  let n = if i < 26 { str_slice("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz", i, i + 1) } else { "t{int_to_str(i)}" }
  if string_in_list(taken, n) { first_untaken(taken, i + 1) } else { n }
}

// A site whose own frame installs no schedule runs under the one its
// callers demand (B4), so only those carry the demand — read once, and
// only when some site needs it.
fn demand_onto(name: String, sites) with !Mutate =
  if any(site_is_seq, sites) { map(site_demanded(demanded_vias(name), ??), sites) } else { sites }

fn site_is_seq(site) = match site {
  WhereSite(s) => schedule_is_seq(s.schedule),
}

fn site_demanded(vias, site) = match site {
  WhereSite(s) => if schedule_is_seq(s.schedule) { WhereSite({...s, vias: vias}) } else { site },
}

fn decl_head(name: String, params, row) with !Mutate =
  "{name}({join(", ", map(param_name, params))}){show_row_suffix(row)}"

// Where the declaration stands, in the developer's coordinates: the module
// its node registered under and the line the parser wrote. A function value
// with no declaration of its own — a constructor, a module let holding a
// closure — has no line to give, and the head stands alone.
fn decl_address(decl) with !Mutate = match decl {
  Some(h) => decl_at(h),
  None => // drift-audit: ignore — no declaration node, so no address: the head is the whole answer
  "",
}

// An op stands where its effect is declared — the effect declaration of that
// name, never a type that shares it (lib/dsp declares a type and an effect
// both named Sample).
fn effect_address(ename: String) with !Mutate = match find(declares_effect(ename, ??), graph_decls_at()) {
  Some(h) => decl_at(h),
  None => // drift-audit: ignore — an effect the substrate registers with no declaration node has no address
  "",
}

fn declares_effect(ename: String, h) = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(EffectDeclStmt(n, _, _)) => n == ename,
  _ => false,
}

fn param_badge(p) with !Mutate = value_badge(param_name(p), param_ty(p))

// SYNTAX's own badge: `s : Float @ f32 (pinned)`. The type names what the
// value is and the width how it is laid out, so a bare-width pin shows its
// base beside the width rather than twice.
fn value_badge(name: String, ty) with !Mutate = "{name} : {value_type(ty)} @ {width_badge(ty)}"

fn value_type(ty) with !Mutate = match chase_type_deep(ty) {
  TReprPin(_, base) => show_type(base),
  shown => show_type(shown),
}

// A width as the gradient settled it. A type that is still a variable has
// no one width: every instantiation is specialized and takes its own (the
// total monomorphization), so the floor the variable would read is not
// what any caller runs. Under `name_type_vars` the variable reads as the
// lowercase name a type parameter is written with — the case rule's own
// spelling, which no judged type holds otherwise.
fn width_badge(ty) with !Mutate = match chase_type_deep(ty) {
  TVar(_) => "per instantiation",
  TName(n, []) => if is_type_param_name(n) { "per instantiation" } else { known_width(TName(n, [])) },
  chased => known_width(chased),
}

fn known_width(ty) with !Mutate = "{repr_name(repr_of(ty))} ({pin_word(ty_has_repr_pin(ty))})"

// A declaration's body: the expression its function holds, so the installs
// and fanout sites `where` lists are the ones under the head it prints. A
// list: none, or one.
fn decl_body(decl) with !Mutate = match decl {
  Some(h) => match graph_node_body(h) {
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, body)) => [node_handle(body)],
    _ => [],
  },
  None => [],
}

// A name with no global entry is a LOCAL: a parameter, a let, a pattern's
// part, a function literal's parameter. It is found the way the judgment
// drew it — from each reference to the name in the module the question is
// about (`graph_refs_at`) along the reference's link (`graph_link_of`) to
// the cell it reads. One binder answers its badge alone; several, each
// with where it is first read, which is what tells them apart on the page.
// A binder nothing reads has no edge to follow and is not listed.
fn binder_badges(name: String, scope: String) with !Mutate = {
  let reads = graph_refs_at(name) |> filter(read_in(scope, ??)) |> flat_map(linked_read) |> reverse
  match unique(map(read_binder, reads)) {
    [] => QRError("not found: {name} — no declaration of it, and no binder of it read in {scope}"),
    [b] => QRWhere([named_badge(name, b)], []),
    binders => QRWhere(map(binder_line(name, reads, ??), binders), []),
  }
}

// One binder's badge with its own variables named: binders of one name in
// different scopes share no variables, so each line names its own.
fn named_badge(name: String, b) with !Mutate = {
  let cp = graph_push_checkpoint()
  name_type_vars([TVar(b)])
  let line = value_badge(name, TVar(b))
  graph_rollback(cp)
  line
}

fn read_in(scope: String, r) with !Mutate =
  byte_len(scope) == 0 || module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(r)) == scope

fn linked_read(r) with !Mutate = match graph_link_of(r) {
  Some(b) => [(b, r)],
  None => [],
}

fn read_binder(read) = match read {
  (b, _) => b,
}

// The refs column prepends, so the reads arrive reversed into judgment
// order; a binder's first read is the first of its reads.
fn binder_line(name: String, reads, b) with !Mutate = match reads |> filter(read_of(b, ??)) |> head {
  (_, r) => "{named_badge(name, b)} — first read at {show_span(span_of_node_raw(r))}",
}

fn read_of(b, read) = match read {
  (rb, _) => rb == b,
}

// The ADT-roster projection (the confessed missing facet): every
// constructor whose scheme's RESULT is the named type, with its arity
// — the env-read the synth enumeration and the eq sum-leaf already
// trust, projected as lines through the where channel (a roster IS a
// badge list). An empty roster is the honest not-found.
// The ctor-membership predicates: an env entry is a constructor of
// `type_name` when its kind is ConstructorScheme and its judgment's
// RESULT is TName(type_name, _) — nullary ctors carry TName directly,
// fielded ctors carry TFun(_, TName, _). An effect is a sum of requests,
// so its operations are its variants: an entry is one of them when its kind
// is EffectOpScheme naming the effect. They live beside their only
// reader (the variants facet); the manifest link refuses the kernel
// vocabulary module calling upward into the judgment layer.
fn is_ctor_of(kind, scheme, type_name) = match kind {
  ConstructorScheme(_, _) => scheme_result_is(type_name, judgment_ty(scheme)),
  EffectOpScheme(owner, _, _, _) => owner == type_name,
  _ => false,
}

// Stage Law: the type name is configuration, the candidate result type is
// the flowing datum.
fn scheme_result_is(type_name, ty) with Pure = match ty {
  TName(n, _) => n == type_name,
  TFun(_, ret, _) => scheme_result_is(type_name, ret),
  _ => false,
}

fn variants_of(tname: String) = match env_snapshot() {
  Env(entries) => {
    let lines = variant_lines(entries, tname, 0, len(entries), [])
    if len(lines) == 0 {
      QRError("no constructors or operations found for: {tname}")
    } else {
      QRWhere(lines, [])
    }
  },
}

fn variant_lines(entries, tname: String, i, n, acc) = if i >= n {
  acc
} else {
  match list_index(entries, i) {
    (name, scheme, _, kind) => if is_ctor_of(kind, scheme, tname) {
      // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the type's ctor count, not a hot loop
      variant_lines(entries, tname, i + 1, n, acc ++ ["{name}/{int_to_str(ctor_arity(scheme))}"])
    } else {
      variant_lines(entries, tname, i + 1, n, acc)
    },
  }
}

fn ctor_arity(scheme) = match judgment_ty(scheme) {
  TFun(params, _, _) => len(params),
  _ => // drift-audit: ignore — the arity of any non-TFun scheme IS zero (a value takes no params); a true total answer, not a masked case
  0,
}

fn op_resume_ty(scheme) with !Mutate = match chase_type_deep(judgment_ty(scheme)) {
  TFun(_, ret, _) => show_type(ret),
  other => show_type(other),
}

// SYNTAX §«Resume discipline»: ->1 one-shot, ->* multi-shot, ->? either;
// ->0 is Abandon — the arm never resumes, the continuation deadens.
fn disc_glyph(disc) with Pure = match disc {
  OneShot => "->1",
  MultiShot => "->*",
  Either => "->?",
  Abandon => "->0",
}

fn repr_name(r) with Pure = match r {
  RI32 => "i32",
  RI64 => "i64",
  RF64 => "f64",
  RF32 => "f32",
  RV128 => "v128",
}

fn pin_word(pinned) with Pure = if pinned { "pinned" } else { "inferred" }

// The pin sits in the scheme's OWN type chain — an authored TReprPin
// survives inside the alias/refinement wrappers; the chased projection
// answers the WIDTH, this walk answers WHO chose it.
fn ty_has_repr_pin(ty) with Pure = match ty {
  TReprPin(_, _) => true,
  TAlias(_, base) => ty_has_repr_pin(base),
  TRefined(base, _) => ty_has_repr_pin(base),
  _ => false,
}

// The fanout sites of a body, each with the schedule its own frame
// installs: the enclosing `~>` chain outermost first, the innermost
// schedule-class install winning. A function literal's or a nested fn's
// body is another frame, so its sites start a chain of their own.
fn fanout_sites(h, tees) with !Mutate = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  match body {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, r)) => fanout_sites(node_handle(l), push(tees, tee_handler_name(r))),
    NExpr(FanoutExpr(_)) => [where_site(h, Branches(FanDistribute, boundary_width(h)), tees)] ++ child_sites(body, tees),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PFanout(fan), _, _)) => [where_site(h, Branches(fan, boundary_width(h)), tees)] ++ child_sites(body, tees),
    NExpr(CallExpr(f, _)) => if callee_is_fanout(f) {
      [where_site(h, PerElement, tees)] ++ child_sites(body, tees)
    } else {
      child_sites(body, tees)
    },
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(_, lb)) => fanout_sites(node_handle(lb), []),
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, fb)) => fanout_sites(node_handle(fb), []),
    _ => child_sites(body, tees),
  }
}

fn child_sites(body, tees) with !Mutate =
  body_child_handles(body) |> flat_map(fanout_sites(??, tees))

fn where_site(h, shape, tees) with !Mutate =
  WhereSite({schedule: schedule_of(tees), shape: shape, span: span_of_node_raw(h), vias: []})

// The branch edges the judgment drew at the site, one per branch (the
// `><` and `<|` judgments both write a FanoutBoundary). A fanout the
// judgment never reached holds none of its own.
fn boundary_width(h) with !Mutate = match graph_boundary_at(h) {
  FanoutBoundary(edges) => len(edges),
  NoBoundary => 0,
  PendingContinuationBoundary(_, _, _, _) => 0,
  ContinuationBoundary(_) => 0,
  InterpBoundary(_) => 0,
}

// THE CALLERS THAT DEMAND A FN THREADED, by the rule the emit demands its
// schedule twins by: a call standing inside a Thread-class install demands
// its callee, and a demanded fn whose direct-call reach holds a fanout
// (`fanout_reach_ask`, graph.mn — the one home of that rule) hands the
// demand to its direct callees in turn. The seeds are read off the decls
// column; each caller's demand is walked once per fn it reaches.
fn demanded_vias(name: String) with !Mutate = {
  let decls = fanout_decls_index()
  let seeds = fold([], decl_seeds, graph_decls_at())
  seeds |> map(seed_via) |> unique |> filter(demand_reaches(decls, seeds, name, ??))
}

// Every (callee, via) a Thread-class install demands directly: the calls
// standing in the extent of such an install, each paired with the fn whose
// frame holds the install.
fn decl_seeds(acc, h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(fname, _, _, _, body)) => extent_calls(node_handle(body), [], fname, acc),
  _ => acc,
}

fn extent_calls(h, tees, via: String, acc) with !Mutate = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  match body {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, r)) => extent_calls(node_handle(l), push(tees, tee_handler_name(r)), via, acc),
    NExpr(CallExpr(f, _)) => fold(seed_call(acc, f, tees, via), extent_calls_step(tees, via), body_child_handles(body)),
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(_, _)) => acc,
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, _)) => acc,
    _ => fold(acc, extent_calls_step(tees, via), body_child_handles(body)),
  }
}

fn extent_calls_step(tees, via: String, acc, h) with !Mutate = extent_calls(h, tees, via, acc)

fn seed_call(acc, f, tees, via: String) with !Mutate = if schedule_is_threaded(schedule_of(tees)) {
  match f {
    N(NExpr(VarRef(callee)), _, _) => push(acc, (callee, via)),
    _ => acc,
  }
} else {
  acc
}

fn seed_via(seed) = match seed {
  (_, via) => via,
}

// Whether `via`'s demand reaches `name`: a round asks the reach rule of
// every callee not yet walked, the ones whose reach holds a fanout hand the
// demand to their direct callees, and the walk ends when a round meets
// `name` or adds nothing new.
fn demand_reaches(decls, seeds, name: String, via: String) with !Mutate = demand_round(
  decls,
  name,
  seeds |> filter(seeded_by(via, ??)) |> map(seed_callee),
  {memo: smap_new(), walked: smap_new()}
)

fn seeded_by(via: String, seed) = match seed {
  (_, v) => str_eq(v, via),
}

fn seed_callee(seed) = match seed {
  (callee, _) => callee,
}

fn demand_round(decls, name: String, frontier, st) with !Mutate = match fold((false, [], st), demand_step(decls, name, ??, ??), frontier) {
  (true, _, _) => true,
  (false, [], _) => false,
  (false, next, st2) => demand_round(decls, name, next, st2),
}

fn demand_step(decls, name: String, acc, callee: String) with !Mutate = match acc {
  (true, _, _) => acc,
  (false, next, st) => if smap_has(st.walked, callee) {
    acc
  } else {
    match fanout_reach_ask(st.memo, decls, callee) {
      (found, memo) => {
        let walked = smap_add(st.walked, callee, 1)
        if found && str_eq(callee, name) {
          (true, next, {memo: memo, walked: walked})
        } else if found {
          (false, fold(next, push, direct_callees(decls, callee)), {memo: memo, walked: walked})
        } else {
          (false, next, {memo: memo, walked: walked})
        }
      },
    }
  },
}

// The names a declaration calls directly, at its own frame (`fanout_fact`,
// the pass the reach rule itself reads).
fn direct_callees(decls, name: String) with !Mutate = match smap_get(decls, name) {
  Some(h) => match graph_node_body(h) {
    NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, body)) => match fanout_fact((false, []), node_handle(body)) {
      (_, callees) => callees,
    },
    _ => [],
  },
  None => [],
}

// The capability-at-tee projection (§11 6.3's felt face): every `~>`
// install in a body rendered with the effects its handler answers — the
// modality as a DERIVED badge (output, never input), read off the
// handler's arms by the judgment's own reader (`handler_effect_names`).
fn tee_lines(h) with !Mutate = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  match body {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, r)) => tee_line(h, tee_handler_name(r)) ++ tee_lines(node_handle(l)),
    _ => body_child_handles(body) |> flat_map(tee_lines),
  }
}

// A handler declaration's line: what its arms absorb and what they answer —
// the answer under the names a developer writes, as a function's head is,
// since a handler whose every arm resumes answers each install's body and
// its answer is a variable of its scheme.
fn handler_line(name: String, arms, h) with !Mutate = {
  let cp = graph_push_checkpoint()
  let answer = match handler_answer_of(name) {
    Some(a) => {
      name_type_vars([a])
      ", answers {value_type(a)}"
    },
    None => "",
  }
  graph_rollback(cp)
  "handler {name} absorbs {join(" + ", handler_effect_names(arms))}{answer}{decl_at(h)}"
}

// An install's line in a function's badges: the handler, what it absorbs
// and what this install answers — the tee's own type, the body's answer met
// by the arms'.
fn tee_line(h, hn: String) with !Mutate = if byte_len(hn) == 0 {
  []
} else {
  match handler_arms(hn) {
    Some(arms) => ["~> {hn} absorbs {join(" + ", handler_effect_names(arms))}, answers {value_type(lookup_ty(h))} at {show_span(span_of_node_raw(h))}"],
    None => ["~> {hn} at {show_span(span_of_node_raw(h))} — no handler declaration by that name"],
  }
}

fn handler_arms(hname: String) = match env_lookup(hname) {
  Some(entry) => match entry {
    (_, _, HandlerKind(_, _, _, _, arms)) => Some(arms),
    _ => None,
  },
  None => None,
}

// The schedule a site runs under: the innermost install whose class is a
// schedule, read off the roster the judgment prices an install by and the
// lowering lowers it by (`projection_of_effects`); Seq when none is.
fn schedule_of(tees) = fold(Seq, inner_schedule, tees)

fn inner_schedule(acc, hname: String) = match handler_arms(hname) {
  Some(arms) => match projection_of_effects(handler_effect_names(arms)) {
    PSchedule(s) => s,
    PDispatch => acc,
    PDerivative => acc,
    PArena => acc,
  },
  None => acc,
}

fn schedule_is_seq(s) = match s {
  Seq => true,
  Threaded => false,
  Simd => false,
  Gpu => false,
  Persisted => false,
}

fn schedule_is_threaded(s) = match s {
  Threaded => true,
  Seq => false,
  Simd => false,
  Gpu => false,
  Persisted => false,
}

// The schedule as `where` names it (SYNTAX's `>< [Thread ×4]`).
fn schedule_word(s) = match s {
  Seq => "Seq",
  Threaded => "Thread",
  Simd => "Simd",
  Gpu => "Gpu",
  Persisted => "Persisted",
}

// A decl's site: its own module beside its own span, both read from the
// columns the parser wrote. The span is the mint fact — a chased reason
// answers the class representative's position, and a type declaration's
// node is never bound at all, so its reason carries none.
fn decl_site_of(handle) with !Mutate =
  (module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(handle)), parse_span_of(handle))

// Every name a declaration declares, each at the declaration's site: its
// own (`body_decl_name`, the one reader of it), then a type's constructors,
// an effect's operations and a handler's state fields — the names a program
// can say that this declaration minted.
fn decl_names_at(handle) with !Mutate = {
  let body = graph_node_body(handle)
  [body_decl_name(body)] ++ inner_decl_names(body) |> map(named_at(decl_site_of(handle)))
}

fn named_at(site, name) = (name, site)

fn inner_decl_names(body) = match body {
  NStmt(TypeDefStmt(_, variants)) => map(variant_name, variants),
  NStmt(EffectDeclStmt(_, _, ops)) => map(op_decl_name, ops),
  NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(_, _, _, states, _, _)) => map(state_field_name, states),
  _ => [],
}

fn variant_name(v) = match v {
  (name, _, _) => name,
}

fn op_decl_name(op) = match op {
  (name, _, _, _) => name,
}

fn span_of_reason(reason) = match reason {
  Located(sp, _) => sp,
  _ => span_zero(),
}

// ─── Which handler declarations provide OP ─────────────────────────
//
// Each handler declaration is noted in the decls column, whose superseded
// generations are already dropped, and its arms name the ops it answers
// (the handler chain IS the capability stack, PLAN §2). The answer is every
// declaration with an arm for OP, at the arm's own line; which install
// serves a given perform is the caret's Handler facet.
fn handlers_providing_op(op_name: String) with !Mutate =
  flat_map(arms_for_op(op_name, ??), graph_decls_at())

fn arms_for_op(op_name: String, h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(hname, _, _, _, arms, _)) => arms |> filter(arm_answers(op_name, ??)) |> map(arm_site(hname, ??)),
  _ => [],
}

fn arm_answers(op_name: String, arm) = arm.op_name == op_name

fn arm_site(hname: String, arm) with !Mutate = (hname, decl_at(node_handle(arm.body)))

// ─── Every value written into a handler's state field ──────────────
//
// The judgment notes a state field's writers on its init before any arm is
// judged (`StateFact`): the init and each `resume … with` update of the
// field — and every value an arm stores into it in place. The facet reads
// that fact for every handler declaring a field of the name, each writer as
// its first source line at its address.
fn state_write_lines(field: String) with !Mutate =
  flat_map(field_write_lines(field, ??), graph_decls_at())

fn field_write_lines(field: String, h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(hname, _, _, states, _, _)) => states |> filter(state_named(field, ??)) |> flat_map(state_writes(hname, ??)),
  _ => [],
}

fn state_named(field: String, st) = st.name == field

fn state_writes(hname: String, st) with !Mutate = match graph_binder_of(node_handle(st.init)) {
  Some(StateFact(f)) => ["{hname}.{st.name}: {int_to_str(len(f.writes))} write(s), {int_to_str(len(f.stores))} in-place store(s)"] ++ map(write_line, f.writes) ++ map(store_line, f.stores),
  _ => ["{hname}.{st.name}: no writers noted — the handler was never judged"],
}

fn write_line(w) with !Mutate =
  "  {head(split("\n", render_body_tokens(graph_node_body(w))))}{decl_at(w)}"

// An in-place store, as the value it stores (or the argument a callee
// stores through).
fn store_line(c) with !Mutate =
  "  store {head(split("\n", render_body_tokens(graph_node_body(c))))}{decl_at(c)}"

// ─── Span → name — the inverse use-edge read (goto/rename anchor) ──
//
// The tightest node enclosing a span is a VarRef whose name the parser wrote
// live; name_at_span reads it (one find_tightest + one graph_node_body), the
// inverse of refs_of_name. None when the span encloses no identifier. This is
// the resolution goto-definition / find-references start from — read live, no
// position→symbol side-index.
fn name_at_span(span) with !Mutate = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(_, span_index) => match find_tightest(span_index, span, None, 999999999) {
    None => None,
    Some(handle) => match graph_node_body(handle) {
      NExpr(VarRef(name)) => Some(name),
      _ => None,
    },
  },
}

// defining_span_of — the span where NAME is bound, read from its env entry's
// Reason (the parser/infer wrote Located(span,_) at the binding). The graph
// already proved where each name lives; goto-definition follows that edge, it
// does not re-scan. None when the name is unbound or its reason is unlocated.
fn defining_span_of(name) with !Mutate = match env_lookup(name) {
  None => None,
  Some(entry) => match entry {
    (_, reason, _) => match reason {
      Located(sp, _) => Some(sp),
      _ => None,
    },
  },
}

// ─── Span Index Search (Arc F.2) ───────────────────────────────────
// The param is BARE Span — the demand law's dual face (never demand a
// proof the body doesn't use): the area fold is total arithmetic over
// the four fields, and the former ValidSpan param converted every
// bare-span caller into ceremony debt (seven obligations at the
// address tie-break alone, measured 2026-07-30) while the proof was
// never read.
fn span_area(s) with Pure = match s {
  Span(sl, sc, el, ec) => if sl == el { ec - sc } else { (el - sl) * 1000 + ec - sc },
}

fn find_tightest(ref idx, target, best_h, best_area) = if len(idx) == 0 {
  best_h
} else {
  match last(idx) {
    (s, h) => if span_contains(s, target) {
      let area = span_area(s)
      if area < best_area {
        find_tightest(drop_last(idx), target, Some(h), area)
      } else {
        find_tightest(drop_last(idx), target, best_h, best_area)
      }
    } else {
      find_tightest(drop_last(idx), target, best_h, best_area)
    },
  }
}

// ── DAG-homed here (the import-truth sweep, 2026-07-23): each fn below
// moved from a module DOWNSTREAM of its users so every user reaches it
// through a forward import edge — the manifest tells the truth.
fn why_expand(reason, depth) = if depth > 20 {
  reason
} else {
  match reason {
    Located(span, inner) => Located(span, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    Unified(a, b) => Unified(why_expand(a, depth + 1), why_expand(b, depth + 1)),
    OpConstraint(op, l, r) => OpConstraint(op, why_expand(l, depth + 1), why_expand(r, depth + 1)),
    VarLookup(name, inner) => VarLookup(name, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    FnReturn(name, inner) => FnReturn(name, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    FnParam(name, idx, inner) => FnParam(name, idx, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    MatchBranch(a, b) => MatchBranch(why_expand(a, depth + 1), why_expand(b, depth + 1)),
    ListElement(inner) => ListElement(why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    IfBranch(inner) => IfBranch(why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    LetBinding(name, inner) => LetBinding(name, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    Instantiation(name, inner) => Instantiation(name, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    Accepted(text, proof, inner) => // The accept edge expands its BINDING side only: the proof is the
    // medium's own sentence at proposal time, carried as it was said —
    // a fact of the generation that proposed, never re-chased in the
    // one that accepted.
    Accepted(text, proof, why_expand(inner, depth + 1)),
    Fresh(id) => match graph_chase(id) {
      GNode(_, resolved) => match resolved {
        Fresh(_) => reason,
        _ => why_expand(resolved, depth + 1),
      },
    },
    _ => reason,
  }
}

// Fill a typed hole with THE sole proven survivor from the Synth chain,
// returned WITH its Reason. The allowed row is the Context's — the
// enclosing decl's AUTHORED constraint (re-deriving it from the hole's
// own type was the wrong-row-source class's second instance). Exactly one
// survivor is the medium's answer; two or more is the tie that TEACHES —
// indexing [0] was a hidden first-wins ranking, a guess between proven
// survivors the tie-break law forbids (PLAN §5).

